1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) of unit squares, where \\(n=2025\\).\nMatilda chooses exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column; denote its position in row \\(i\\) by the column \\(\\pi(i)\\).  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation.\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\qquad\\text{if }\\pi(i)>1,\n\\qquad\nr_i=(i,\\pi(i)+1)\\qquad\\text{if }\\pi(i)<n .\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{\\ell_i\\mid \\pi(i)>1\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{r_i\\mid \\pi(i)<n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause exactly one row has \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) and exactly one row has \\(\\pi(i)=n\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n|L|=|R|=n-1,\\qquad L\\cap R=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nNo rectangle whose interior contains no uncovered square can contain two distinct cells of \\(L\\cup R\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(Q=I\\times J\\) (with \\(I,J\\) intervals of rows and columns) contains two distinct cells of \\(L\\cup R\\).\n\n*Case 1:* both cells belong to \\(R\\).  \nThen there exist rows \\(i\\neq i'\\) with \\(\\pi(i)<n,\\ \\pi(i')<n\\) such that\n\\(r_i=(i,\\pi(i)+1)\\in Q\\) and \\(r_{i'}=(i',\\pi(i')+1)\\in Q\\).\nWithout loss of generality \\(\\pi(i)+1\\le\\pi(i')+1\\); hence\n\\(J\\) contains the whole interval \\([\\pi(i)+1,\\pi(i')+1]\\) and therefore also the column \\(\\pi(i')\\).\nSince \\(i'\\in I\\), the hole \\((i',\\pi(i'))\\) lies inside \\(Q\\), contradicting the hypothesis.\n\n*Case 2:* both cells belong to \\(L\\).  \nThe argument is symmetric: if \\(\\ell_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) and \\(\\ell_{i'}=(i',\\pi(i')-1)\\) are in \\(Q\\),\nthen \\(J\\) contains the interval \\([\\pi(i)-1,\\pi(i')-1]\\) and consequently the column \\(\\pi(i)\\);\nthe hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) is then inside \\(Q\\), a contradiction.\n\n*Case 3:* one cell is from \\(L\\) and the other from \\(R\\).  \nSuppose \\(\\ell_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\in Q\\) and \\(r_{i'}=(i',\\pi(i')+1)\\in Q\\).\nIf \\(\\pi(i)-1\\le\\pi(i')+1\\) then \\(J\\) contains the column \\(\\pi(i)\\) (the hole of row \\(i\\));\nif \\(\\pi(i')+1\\le\\pi(i)-1\\) then \\(J\\) contains the column \\(\\pi(i')\\) (the hole of row \\(i'\\)).\nIn either sub‑case a hole lies inside \\(Q\\), contradicting the hypothesis.\n\nAll possibilities lead to a contradiction; therefore a rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(L\\cup R\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound\n\nEvery cell of \\(L\\cup R\\) must be covered, because all cells except the uncovered ones are tiled.\nBy Lemma 1 each tile can cover at most one element of \\(L\\cup R\\).\nUsing (1),\n\n\\[\n|L\\cup R|=|L|+|R|=2(n-1)\n\\]\n\nimplies that any tiling uses at least \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{t\\ge 2n-2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the uncovered squares on the main diagonal:\n\\[\n\\pi(i)=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal rectangle\n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich covers the right‑hand part of row \\(i\\).\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical rectangle\n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich covers the lower part of column \\(j\\).\n\nThe sets \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint,\nnone of them contains a diagonal square \\((i,i)\\), and together they cover every off‑diagonal square.\nHence this construction uses exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Minimality\n\nEquation (2) gives a universal lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles,\nand the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.\nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
