\}\\) of unit squares, where \\(n=2025\\).\nMatilda chooses exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column; denote its position in row \\(i\\) by the column \\(\\pi(i)\\).  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation.\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\qquad\\text{if }\\pi(i)>1,\n\\qquad\nr_i=(i,\\pi(i)+1)\\qquad\\text{if }\\pi(i)<n .\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{\\ell_i\\mid \\pi(i)>1\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{r_i\\mid \\pi(i)<n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause exactly one row has \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) and exactly one row has \\(\\pi(i)=n\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n|L|=|R|=n-1,\\qquad L\\cap R=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nNo rectangle whose interior contains no uncovered square can contain two distinct cells of \\(L\\cup R\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(Q=I\\times J\\) (with \\(I,J\\) intervals of rows and columns) contains two distinct cells of \\(L\\cup R\\).\n\n*Case 1:* both cells belong to \\(R\\).  \nThen there exist rows \\(i\\neq i'\\) with \\(\\pi(i)<n,\\ \\pi(i')<n\\) such that\n\\(r_i=(i,\\pi(i)+1)\\in Q\\) and \\(r_{i'}=(i',\\pi(i')+1)\\in Q\\).\nWithout loss of generality \\(\\pi(i)+1\\le\\pi(i')+1\\); hence\n\\(J\\) contains the whole interval \\([\\pi(i)+1,\\pi(i')+1]\\) and therefore also the column \\(\\pi(i')\\).\nSince \\(i'\\in I\\), the hole \\((i',\\pi(i'))\\) lies inside \\(Q\\), contradicting the hypothesis.\n\n*Case 2:* both cells belong to \\(L\\).  \nThe argument is symmetric: if \\(\\ell_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) and \\(\\ell_{i'}=(i',\\pi(i')-1)\\) are in \\(Q\\),\nthen \\(J\\) contains the interval \\([\\pi(i)-1,\\pi(i')-1]\\) and consequently the column \\(\\pi(i)\\);\nthe hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) is then inside \\(Q\\), a contradiction.\n\n*Case 3:* one cell is from \\(L\\) and the other from \\(R\\).  \nSuppose \\(\\ell_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\in Q\\) and 