ument applies to the remaining diagram.\n\n6. **Applying Lemma 2.**  \n   For \\(L\\) we have \\(m=n-1\\); thus any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  By symmetry the same holds for \\(R\\).  Consequently any tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   rectangles.\n\n7. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(f(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \n   - For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n     \\[\n     H_i=\\{(i,j):j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     covering the entire right segment of row \\(i\\).  The \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) are disjoint and together cover all squares of \\(R\\).  \n   - For each \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n     \\[\n     V_j=\\{(i,j-1):i=j,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     covering the entire left segment of column \\(j-1\\).  The \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) are disjoint and together cover all squares of \\(L\\).  \n\n   The two families are disjoint because \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).  Hence we obtain a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n8. **Numerical answer.**  \n   With \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n9. **Explicit sub‑task (one tile).**  \n   Suppose a single rectangle \\(R_0\\) were used.  In each row the uncovered square must lie either to the left of \\(R_0\\) or to the right of \\(R_0\\).  Consequently all uncovered squares would lie in at most two columns (the leftmost column not covered by \\(R_0\\) and the rightmost column not covered by \\(R_0\\)).  This contradicts the requirement that each of the \\(2025\\) columns contain exactly one uncovered square.  Hence a single tile cannot satisfy the condition.\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  For each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(f(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(f\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); its inverse \\(g\\) satisfies \\(g(j)=f^{-1}(j)\\).\n\nDefine the two subsets of covered squares\n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j<f(i)\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j>f(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L\\) consists of all squares lying strictly to the left of the hole in their row, and \\(R\\) consists of those lying strictly to the right.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \n*Every axis‑parallel rectangle consisting solely of covered squares is contained entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a rectangle \\(Q\\) intersect a row \\(i\\).  Its column interval is some \\([c_1,c_2]\\) with \\(c_1\\le c_2\\).  If \\(c_1\\le f(i)\\le c_2\\) then \\((i,f(i))\\) would belong to \\(Q\\), contradicting that \\(Q\\) contains only covered squares.  Hence either \\(c_2<f(i)\\) (the whole rectangle lies left of the hole in row \\(i\\)) or \\(c_1>f(i)\\) (the whole rectangle lies right of the hole).  Since the column interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\) is the same for all rows intersected by \\(Q\\), the same inequality holds for every such row; consequently all squares of \\(Q\\) lie either in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause of Lemma 1, a tiling of all covered squares splits into a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\).  We now obtain a lower bound for each part.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \n*Let \\(D\\) be a Ferrers diagram (Young diagram) whose rows, after ordering from top to bottom, have distinct positive lengths \\(\\ell_1<\\ell_2<\\dots <\\ell_m\\).  Any partition of \\(D\\) into axis‑parallel rectangles uses at least \\(m\\) rectangles.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the rightmost column of \\(D\\).  By the distinct‑length hypothesis this column contains exactly