st column, which contains a single square belonging to the longest row; that square must lie in a rectangle that does not involve any other row, allowing the induction step.\n\n6. **Application of Lemma 2.**  \n   For \\(L\\) we have \\(m=n-1\\); therefore any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  By symmetry the same lower bound holds for \\(R\\).  Consequently any tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]  \n   rectangles.\n\n7. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(f(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \n   - For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n     \\[\n     H_i=\\{(i,j):j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]  \n     covering the entire right segment of row \\(i\\).  \n   - For each \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n     \\[\n     V_j=\\{(i,j-1):i=j,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]  \n     covering the entire left segment of column \\(j-1\\).  \n   The families \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover all squares of \\(R\\) and \\(L\\) respectively, giving exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n8. **Numerical answer.**  \n   With \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n   \\]\n\n9. **Impossibility of a single tile.**  \n   If only one rectangle were used, all uncovered squares would have to lie either to its left or to its right, so they could occupy at most two columns.  This contradicts the requirement that each of the \\(2025\\) columns contain exactly one uncovered square.  Hence a single tile cannot satisfy the conditions.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(f(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\nf:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis a 