ts ,n\\}\\); the square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) is the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) and column \\(\\pi(i)\\).\n\nDefine two subsets of the board:\n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\; \\pi(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L\\) consists of all unit squares lying **left** of the uncovered squares, and \\(R\\) consists of all unit squares lying **right** of them.  \nThe sets \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint and together contain all covered squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound\n\nConsider the set \\(L\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i = |\\{j\\mid (i,j)\\in L\\}| = \\pi(i)-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, the numbers \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) are precisely the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\); in particular they are all distinct.\n\nLet \\(i\\) be a row with \\(\\ell_i>0\\) (i.e. \\(\\pi(i)>1\\)).  \nThe square \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) is the **rightmost** covered square in row \\(i\\).  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(T\\) of the tiling contains \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) and also contains the rightmost square \\((k,\\ell_k)\\) of another row \\(k\\neq i\\) with \\(\\ell_k\\neq\\ell_i\\).  \nWithout loss of generality assume \\(\\ell_i<\\ell_k\\).  \nSince \\(T\\) is axis‑parallel, its width must be at least \\(\\ell_k\\); consequently \\(T\\) would also contain the square \\((i,\\ell_i+1)\\).  \nBut \\((i,\\ell_i+1)=(i,\\pi(i))\\) is an uncovered square, contradicting the requirement that tiles cover only covered squares.  \n\nHence **no rectangle can contain the rightmost covered squares of two distinct rows**.  \nEvery row with \\(\\ell_i>0\\) therefore contributes a distinct tile.  \nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) such rows (all rows except the one with \\(\\pi(i)=1\\)).  \nConsequently any tiling of \\(L\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\nThe same argument applied to the set \\(R\\) (where the rightmost covered square of a row is the leftmost one) shows that any tiling of \\(R\\) also requires at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\nSince the tiles covering \\(L\\) and those covering \\(R\\) are disjoint, the whole board needs at least\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.  This lower bound holds for **every** permutation \\(\\pi\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An explicit construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nThus the uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\), one in each row and column.\n\n*Left of the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place a tile\n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{vert}}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{\\text{vert}}_j\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\); it covers precisely the squares of column \\(j\\) that lie below the diagonal.  \nThe \\(n-1\\) tiles \\(\\{T^{\\text{vert}}_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) cover the entire set \\(L\\).\n\n*Right of the diagonal.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place a tile\n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{horiz}}_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{\\text{horiz}}_i\\) is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\); it covers precisely the squares of row \\(i\\) that lie to the right of the diagonal.  \nThe \\(n-1\\) tiles \\(\\{T^{\\text{horiz}}_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) cover the entire set \\(R\\).\n\nAll tiles are disjoint, each lies entirely on grid lines, and no tile contains an uncovered square.  \nThe uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\); each row and each column contains precisely one such square.\n\nThe total number of tiles used is\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same value; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given size \\(n=2025\