 perhaps there is a rectangle that includes left square from row i and right square from row k but does not include any uncovered squares because the uncovered squares are outside the column interval. Let's try i=3, k=4, pi(i)=3, pi(k)=4 (identity). L square: (3,2) (since 2<3). R square: (4,5) (since 5>4). Then rectangle rows 3..4, columns 2..5. Uncovered squares: (3,3) and (4,4). Both are in rectangle (col 3 and 4). So fails.\n\nIf we try a non-identity permutation, maybe we can find a rectangle that includes left and right squares but not uncovered squares. Let's try n=5, permutation pi = (2,1,4,3,5). So uncovered squares: (1,2), (2,1), (3,4), (4,3), (5,5). L squares: for each row i, columns < pi(i). R squares: columns > pi(i). Let's pick left square from row i=5: pi(5)=5, L squares: none (since pi(5)-1 =4, L squares are columns 1..4). So left square (5,4) is left. Right square from row i=1: pi(1)=2, R squares: columns >2, e.g., (1,5). So rectangle rows 1..5, columns 4..5? Actually left square column 4, right square column 5, rows 1..5, columns 4..5. Does rectangle include any uncovered squares? Uncovered squares: (1,2) column 2 not in rectangle, (2,1) column 1 not in rectangle, (3,4) column 4 row 3 is in rectangle (row 3 col 4). So uncovered square (3,4) is covered. So fails.\n\nIt seems plausible that any rectangle containing a left square and a right square will also contain some uncovered square, but we need to be rigorous. The solution's argument only considered same row case. But maybe it's still true for any rectangle. Let's attempt to prove: Suppose rectangle T includes a left square (i, j_L) with j_L < pi(i) and a right square (k, j_R) with j_R > pi(k). Without loss, assume i <= k (swap if needed). Then rectangle includes rows i..k and columns j_L..j_R. Since j_L < pi(i), we have pi(i) >= j_L+1. Since j_R > pi(k), we have pi(k) <= j_R-1. So pi(i) is at least j_L+1, pi(k) is at most j_R-1. So the interval [j_L, j_R] includes both pi(i) and pi(k) (since pi(i) >= j_L+1 <= j_R? Wait we need to check if pi(i) <= j_R. Since pi(i) could be > j_R? Possibly if pi(i) is large. But we have j_R > pi(k). pi(i) could be > pi(k) or < pi(k). Let's examine both cases.\n\nCase 1: pi(i) <= pi(k). Then pi(i) <= pi(k) < j_R, and pi(i) > j_L (since j_L < pi(i)). So pi(i) is in interval [j_L+1, j_R-1] thus inside rectangle. So uncovered square (i, pi(i)) is covered.\n\nCase 2: pi(i) > pi(k). Then pi(k) < pi(i). Since j_R > pi(k), we have pi(k) < j_R. But pi(i) could be > j_R. However, we also have j_L < pi(i). So pi(i) > j_L. So pi(i) might be > j_R, meaning pi(i) not in interval. But pi(k) is less than pi(i). Since pi(k) >? Actually pi(k) < pi(i). But pi(k) could be less than j_L? Possibly. Let's see: j_L < pi(i). But j_L could be > pi(k). For rectangle to contain left square (i, j_L) where j_L < pi(i). j_L could be any column less than pi(i). Could be greater than pi(k) or less. If j_L > pi(k), then pi(k) is not in interval [j_L, j_R] because pi(k) < j_L. So rectangle may not contain uncovered square (k, pi(k)). However, we need to check if any row r between i and k has pi(r) in interval. Since pi is a permutation, the set of pi values for rows i..k is a set of size (k-i+1). The interval [j_L, j_R] has length j_R - j_L + 1. Since we have at least two values (j_L and j_R) that are outside the pi values (j_L < pi(i), j_R > pi(k)), it's plausible that the interval covers at least one pi(r). But not guaranteed.\n\nConsider a small example to test: n=4, permutation pi = (2,3,4,1). So uncovered squares: (1,2), (2,3), (3,4), (4,1). L squares: row1: col1; row2: col1,2; row3: col1,2,3; row4: none (since pi(4)=1). R squares: row1: col3,4; row2: col4; row3: none; row4: col2,3,4.\n\nPick left square (row2, col1) (since col1 < pi(2)=3). Pick right square (row4, col4) (col4 > pi(4)=1). Rectangle rows 2..4, columns 1..4 (full board rows 2-4). This includes uncovered squares (2,3) and (3,4) and (4,1). So fails.\n\nPick left square (row3, col2) (col2<pi(3)=4). Right square (row1, col4) (col4>pi(1)=2). Rectangle rows 1..3, columns 2..4. Uncovered squares: (1,2) col2 row1 is in rectangle? Yes, col2 row1 is in rectangle (since rows 1..3, cols 2..4). So fails.\n\nPick left square (row4, col? none). So no left.\n\nThus seems any rectangle containing left and right squares includes uncovered squares. But we need a rigorous proof. The solution's argument only considered same row case, but perhaps it's enough because any rectangle containing left and right squares must have at least one row where both left and right squares appear? Actually a rectangle could contain left squares from some rows and right squares from other rows, but not necessarily both in same row. However, the rectangle's shape is axis-aligned; if it contains a left square from row i and a right square from row k, then for any row r between i and k, the rectangle includes columns from j_L to j_R. Since j_L < pi(i) and j_R > pi(k), we need to see if there is some r such that pi(r) lies between j_L and j_R. Since pi is a permutation, the values pi(i) and pi(k) are distinct. The interval [j_L, j_R] includes all columns between j_L and j_R inclusive. Since j_L < pi(i) and pi(k) < j_R, the interval includes at least the set {j_L+1,...,j_R-1}. The values pi(i) and pi(k) are within that interval? Actually pi(i) > j_L, but could be > j_R. pi(k) < j_R, but could be < j_L. So it's possible that pi(i) > j_R and pi(k) < j_L. In that case, neither pi(i) nor pi(k) is inside interval. But we need to consider other rows r. Could there be a row r where pi(r) is between j_L and j_R? Possibly. But not guaranteed. Let's try to construct a counterexample where rectangle contains left square (i, j_L) and right square (k, j_R) but no uncovered squares. That would be a counterexample to the claim that no tile can contain squares from L and R simultaneously.\n\nWe need to find a permutation pi and indices i, k, j_L, j_R such that j_L < pi(i) and j_R > pi(k), and for all rows r between i and k inclusive, pi(r) is either <= j_L or >= j_R (i.e., not between). That would ensure no uncovered squares are inside rectangle.\n\nLet's try small n=5. Permutation pi = (5,1,2,3,4). So uncovered squares: (1,5), (2,1), (3,2), (4,3), (5,4). L squares: row1: columns 1..4 (since pi(1)=5). Row2: none (pi(2)=1). Row3: column1 (since pi(3)=2). Row4: columns1,2 (since pi(4)=3). Row5: columns1,2,3 (since pi(5)=4). R squares: row1: none (since pi(1)=5). Row2: columns2..5 (since pi(2)=1). Row3: columns3..5 (since pi(3)=2). Row4: columns4..5 (since pi(4)=3). Row5: column5 (since pi(5)=4).\n\nNow pick left square (row5, col3) (since col3 < pi(5)=4). That's in L. Pick right square (row2, col5) (col5 > pi(2)=1). That's in R. Rectangle rows 2..5, columns 3..5. Let's see uncovered squares in that rectangle: (2,1) not in rectangle (col1). (3,2) not in rectangle (col2). (4,3) row4 col3 is in rectangle! Indeed (4,3) is uncovered (since pi(4)=3). So fails.\n\nTry left square (row3, col1) (since col1 < pi(3)=2). Right square (row2, col4) (col4 > pi(2)=1). Rectangle rows 2..3, columns 1..4. Uncovered squares: (2,1) is in rectangle (row2 col1). So fails.\n\nTry left square (row4, col2) (col2 < pi(4)=3). Right square (row2, col3) (col3 > pi(2)=1). Rectangle rows 2..4, columns 2..3. Uncovered squares: (2,1) not in rectangle, (3,2) row3 col2 is uncovered? pi(3)=2, so uncovered square (3,2) is in rectangle (row3 col2). So fails.\n\nThus far no counterexample.\n\nBut we need to be thorough: The claim might be true for any permutation. Let's attempt to prove: Suppose rectangle includes left square (i, j_L) with j_L < pi(i) and right square (k, j_R) with j_R > pi(k). Let a = min(i,k), b = max(i,k). Then rectangle includes rows a..b and columns j_L..j_R. For each row r in [a,b], we consider pi(r). Since pi is a permutation, the values pi(r) for r in [a,b] are distinct. The interval [j_L, j_R] has length at least 2 (since j_L < j_R). The set of pi(r) for r in [a,b] has size (b-a+1). If (b-a+1) > (j_R - j_L + 1), then by pigeonhole principle some pi(r) must be outside interval, but we need at least one inside. Actually we need at least one pi(r) inside interval. If the number of rows is greater than the number of columns in interval, then some pi(r) must be outside interval, not helpful. But if the number of rows is less than or equal to interval length, then it's possible that all pi(r) are outside interval. However, we have constraints: For row i, pi(i) > j_L (since j_L < pi(i)). For row k, pi(k) < j_R (since j_R > pi(k)). So at least for rows i and k, pi(i) and pi(k) are on opposite sides of the interval's interior. But they could be outside the interval: pi(i) could be > j_R (i.e., to the right of interval), and pi(k) could be < j_L (to the left). In that case, both are outside interval. But we need to check if there exists any row r between i and k where pi(r) lies inside interval. Not guaranteed.\n\nConsider a permutation where pi(i) is far right and pi(k) is far left, and all other pi(r) are also far left or far right, avoiding the interval. For example, n=6, choose pi such that pi(1)=6, pi(2)=5, pi(3)=4, pi(4)=3, pi(5)=2, pi(6)=1 (reverse order). Then left squares are those columns less than pi(i). For row1, left squares columns 1..5 (since pi(1)=6). For row6, left squares none (since pi(6)=1). Right squares: row1 none, row6 columns 2..6 (since pi(6)=1). Let's pick left square (row1, col1) (j_L=1). Right square (row6, col6) (j_R=6). Rectangle rows 1..6, columns 1..6 (full board). Contains uncovered squares (the diagonal). So fails.\n\nTry left square (row2, col1) (since pi(2)=5). Right square (row5, col6) (since pi(5)=2, right squares are columns 3..6). So rectangle rows 2..5, columns 1..6. Contains uncovered squares (2,5) etc. So fails.\n\nThus seems plausible that any rectangle containing left and right squares must contain some uncovered square. But the solution's proof only considered same row case, which is insufficient to guarantee the claim for all rectangles. However, the claim might still be true, but the proof is incomplete. The question is: Does this constitute a \"critical error\" at Level 2 or Level 3? According to the hierarchical decision tree, Level 2 checks reasoning validity based on methods used, not on correctness of individual claims. The method used is \"proof by contradiction\" via case analysis. The solution's proof is incomplete but the method is valid. However, the incomplete proof might be considered a \"critical error\" if the reasoning is invalid. The question is whether the reasoning is valid overall. The solution's claim \"No tile can contain a square of L and a square of R simultaneously\" is a crucial step. If this claim is false, the lower bound argument fails. If the claim is true but the proof is incomplete, the reasoning might still be considered valid if the claim can be justified by a valid method (the solution attempted a proof but gave insufficient justification). According to the guidelines, Level 2 checks whether the solution uses valid mathematical principles, not whether each claim is proven correctly. However, if a claim is false, then the reasoning is invalid. If a claim is unproven but plausible, does that count as invalid reasoning? The guidelines say: \"If the solution uses invalid mathematical principles (e.g., trial-and-error, nonsense) → FAIL.\" It does not say that an incomplete proof is automatically a fail; that's a presentation issue (Level 3). The solution uses a proof by contradiction (valid method) but the proof is incomplete. That might be considered a justification gap (Level 3). However, we need to see if the claim is actually false. If the claim is false, the reasoning is invalid, leading to a false lower bound, making answer possibly wrong. But answer appears correct, so maybe the claim is true. Let's examine more thoroughly.\n\nWe need to prove that any axis-aligned rectangle that contains at least one square from L and at least one square from R must contain at least one uncovered square (i.e., a square from the permutation). This is reminiscent of a property of permutations: the set of points (i, pi(i)) is a \"non-crossing\" monotone path? Actually the set of points forms a permutation matrix. The region left of the points (L) and right of the points (R) are separated by the points. The claim is that any axis-aligned rectangle intersecting both sides must intersect the points. This is reminiscent of the \"separating hyperplane\" property: The points form a monotone increasing curve (if we sort by i). The set of points (i, pi(i)) is a permutation graph; the points are not monotonic in column order unless pi is monotonic. Actually pi could be any permutation, not necessarily monotonic. So the points can be scattered. The region L is the set of squares left of each point in its row; region R is squares right of each point in its row. The union of L and R covers all squares except the permutation points. The claim is that any rectangle intersecting both L and R must intersect the permutation points. Is that always true? Let's think.\n\nConsider a permutation pi: For each row i, we have a vertical line at column pi(i) that separates left and right. The left region L consists of squares left of that line in each row; right region R consists of squares right of that line. The shape of L is like a Young diagram shape (Ferrers diagram) if we sort rows by pi(i). But pi can be any permutation, so L is not necessarily monotonic across rows. However, each row has a cut point pi(i). So L is a set of squares where column < pi(i). R is column > pi(i). The \"cut\" points form a \"staircase\" shape but not monotonic.\n\nNow consider a rectangle defined by rows a..b and columns c..d. Suppose there is a left square in this rectangle: there exists i in [a,b] and j in [c,d] such that j < pi(i). Similarly, there is a right square: there exists k in [a,b] and l in [c,d] such that l > pi(k). So we have j < pi(i) and l > pi(k). We need to show there exists some r in [a,b] such that c <= pi(r) <= d (i.e., the rectangle includes a point (r, pi(r))). Actually we need to show there exists a row r where pi(r) is within [c,d] and also the column pi(r) is within rectangle. Since rectangle includes all columns c..d, if pi(r) is between c and d inclusive, then (r, pi(r)) is inside rectangle. So we need to show that the set {pi(r) | r in [a,b]} intersects the interval [c,d]. Equivalent to: The interval [c,d] intersects the set of pi-values for rows in that range.\n\nGiven that there exists i with pi(i) > j >= c (since j is in [c,d] and j < pi(i), so pi(i) > c). Also there exists k with pi(k) < l <= d (since l is in [c,d] and l > pi(k), so pi(k) < d). So we have at least one pi(i) > c and at least one pi(k) < d. But does that guarantee that there is some pi(r) in [c,d]? Not necessarily: pi(i) could be > d, and pi(k) could be < c. Then pi(i) > d and pi(k) < c, and there might be no pi(r) in [c,d] for any r in [a,b]. Let's examine if that scenario can happen.\n\nWe have i and k possibly distinct. Suppose pi(i) > d (so pi(i) is to the right of rectangle). Since j < pi(i) and j is in [c,d], we have j <= d < pi(i). So j is less than pi(i) but still within rectangle. That's okay. Similarly, pi(k) < c (to left of rectangle). Since l > pi(k) and l is in [c,d], we have c <= l <= d, and pi(k) < c <= l. So pi(k) is left of rectangle.\n\nThus we have a left square (i, j) where j is within rectangle but less than pi(i) which is outside rectangle to the right. And a right square (k, l) where l is within rectangle but greater than pi(k) which is left of rectangle. So rectangle includes squares left of pi(i) and right of pi(k) but not the points themselves. Could there be any row r where pi(r) falls inside [c,d]? Possibly not if all pi-values for rows in [a,b] are either < c or > d. But we have at least one pi(i) > d and at least one pi(k) < c. So the set of pi-values for rows in [a,b] includes values on both sides of the interval, but could skip the interval. For example, suppose rows a..b are {1,2}. pi(1)=10, pi(2)=1, rectangle columns c=3, d=5. Then left square: row1, column j=4 (since j<pi(1)=10). Right square: row2, column l=4 (since l>pi(2)=1). So rectangle rows 1..2, columns 3..5 includes left square (1,4) and right square (2,4). Does rectangle contain any uncovered square? Uncovered squares are (1,10) and (2,1). Neither is in rectangle (since columns 10 and 1 are outside). So rectangle contains left and right squares but no uncovered squares. This is a counterexample! Let's test with n >=10. Choose n=10, pi(1)=10, pi(2)=1, and for other rows assign any values not interfering. Then L: row1 columns 1..9; row2 L: none (since pi(2)=1). R: row1 none; row2 columns 2..10. Choose rectangle rows 1..2, columns 3..5. Contains left square (1,4) (since 4<10). Contains right square (2,4) (since 4>1). No uncovered squares inside rectangle. So the claim \"No tile can contain a square of L and a square of R simultaneously\" is false. Indeed, a rectangle can contain left squares from a row where pi(i) is far right and right squares from a row where pi(k) is far left, and the rectangle columns are in between those far extremes, thus not covering any pi(i) or pi(k). This rectangle includes squares from L and R but no uncovered squares. So the claim is false.\n\nThus the solution's Lemma that each tile lies wholly in L or wholly in R is false. This invalidates the lower bound argument that each tile lies wholly in L or wholly in R, and thus the lower bound of 2n-2 may be invalid. However, the solution's lower bound argument also uses Lemma 1 about rightmost covered squares of L cannot be in same tile. That might still hold even if a tile can contain both L and R squares, but the argument that each tile lies wholly in L or wholly in R is used to separate the counting of tiles for L and R. If that claim is false, the lower bound may be weaker: a tile could cover some squares from L and some from R, thus potentially reducing the total number of tiles needed. The lower bound of 2n-2 might not hold. However, the construction achieving 2n-2 shows that 2n-2 is achievable. But is it minimal? Could we achieve fewer tiles by using tiles that cross the diagonal? Possibly. The solution's lower bound may be too high if crossing tiles are allowed. The problem asks for minimum number of tiles; we need to see if a better arrangement exists.\n\nWe need to check if the lower bound argument is indeed flawed. Let's examine the original problem: We have a 2025x2025 grid, we need to place rectangular tiles (axis-aligned) covering unit squares, each unit square covered at most once, such that each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square. So we need to cover all squares except a set of n uncovered squares, one per row and column. The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix. The question: what is the minimum number of tiles needed? The solution claims 2n-2, using vertical and horizontal tiles that each cover one side of the diagonal, not crossing. However, perhaps we can use fewer tiles by using larger rectangles that cross the diagonal and cover both left and right sides, reducing tile count. For example, consider n=2: grid 2x2, need one uncovered per row and column. Uncovered squares must be (1,1) and (2,2) or (1,2) and (2,1). Let's test minimal number of tiles. For n=2, we have 4 squares, need to leave 2 uncovered squares, covering 2 squares. Could we cover the two squares with a single tile? If uncovered squares are (1,1) and (2,2), then the uncovered squares are diagonal, the remaining squares are (1,2) and (2,1), which are not adjacent in a rectangle (they are not contiguous in a rectangle shape because they are not forming a rectangle: they are two squares not sharing a side). So we cannot cover them with one tile because a rectangle covering both would also include (1,1) or (2,2). So we need at least 2 tiles, each covering one of the off-diagonal squares. So for n=2, minimum tiles = 2 = 2n-2 = 2. So matches.\n\nFor n=3: grid 3x3, need 3 uncovered squares, one per row and column. Let's test if we can achieve fewer than 2n-2 = 4 tiles. The solution's construction uses 4 tiles: vertical tiles covering left side (2 tiles) and horizontal tiles covering right side (2 tiles). Could we do with 3 tiles? Let's try to find a configuration. Suppose we pick uncovered squares as (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). Then L squares: (2,1), (3,1), (3,2). R squares: (1,2), (1,3), (2,3). That's 3 left squares and 3 right squares, total 6 squares to cover. Could we cover them with 3 tiles? Possibly using one tile covering (2,1),(3,1),(3,2) (a 2x2 rectangle covering rows 2-3, columns 1-2). That rectangle includes (2,2) which is uncovered, so not allowed. So can't cross diagonal. Could we use a tile covering (2,1) and (3,1) (vertical tile) and another covering (3,2) (single square tile) and then cover right side with maybe 2 tiles? That would be 4 tiles. Could we use a tile covering (1,2),(1,3),(2,3) (L shape? Actually rectangle rows 1-2, columns 2-3 includes (2,2) uncovered, not allowed. So crossing diagonal is problematic.\n\nBut maybe we can choose a different permutation to allow crossing tiles. For n=3, choose uncovered squares (1,2), (2,3), (3,1). Then L squares: row1: column1 (since pi(1)=2, left squares column1). Row2: columns1,2 (since pi(2)=3). Row3: none (since pi(3)=1). R squares: row1: column3 (since pi(1)=2). Row2: none (pi(2)=3). Row3: columns2,3 (since pi(3)=1). So total squares to cover: L squares: (1,1), (2,1), (2,2). R squares: (1,3), (3,2), (3,3). That's 6 squares. Could we cover them with 3 tiles? Possibly using one tile covering (1,1),(2,1),(2,2) (rectangle rows 1-2, columns 1-2). That rectangle includes (1,2) uncovered? Actually (1,2) is uncovered square (since pi(1)=2). The rectangle rows 1-2, columns 1-2 includes (1,2) which is uncovered, not allowed. So can't.\n\nWhat about using tile covering (2,1),(3,2),(3,3) maybe? That's not a rectangle.\n\nThus maybe 4 tiles is minimal for n=3. Let's test n=4: 2n-2 = 6. Could we achieve fewer? Possibly using crossing tiles that cover both left and right squares but avoid uncovered squares. Let's try to find a configuration for n=4 where we can cover with 5 tiles. The uncovered squares must be a permutation. Perhaps we can choose a permutation that allows a large rectangle covering many squares on both sides but not covering any uncovered squares. For a rectangle to avoid uncovered squares, its rows and columns must be such that for each row in the rectangle, the rectangle's column interval does not include the uncovered column for that row. So for each row i in the rectangle, either all columns of rectangle are < pi(i) (i.e., rectangle lies entirely left of uncovered square) or all columns > pi(i) (entirely right). So the rectangle must be entirely on one side of each uncovered square for each row. However, the rectangle could be left of some rows and right of others, as long as it doesn't cross the uncovered column for any row. For a rectangle to contain both left and right squares, there must be at least one row where rectangle is left of pi(i) (so rectangle columns are less than pi(i)), and at least one row where rectangle is right of pi(k) (so rectangle columns are greater than pi(k)). That means the rectangle's column interval must be such that for some rows, the interval is entirely left of pi(i), and for other rows, the interval is entirely right of pi(k). This is possible if the interval lies between pi(k) and pi(i) for some rows, but not crossing any pi values. That is, the interval must be a subset of the complement of the set {pi(i)} across rows. For each row, the rectangle's column interval must be either entirely less than pi(i) or entirely greater than pi(i). So the interval must avoid all pi(i) values for rows in the rectangle. So the rectangle's columns must be a set of columns that does not intersect the set of pi(i) for any row i in the rectangle. That is, the interval must be a contiguous set of columns that avoids all pi(i) for those rows. Since pi(i) values are distinct across rows, we can possibly find an interval that lies between two consecutive pi values (in sorted order) and includes rows whose pi(i) are either all larger or all smaller. But if the interval lies between two pi values, then for rows whose pi(i) are less than the interval, the interval is to the right of pi(i) (so rectangle is right side for those rows). For rows whose pi(i) are greater than the interval, rectangle is left side for those rows. So indeed a rectangle can contain both left and right squares if its column interval lies between two pi values and includes rows on both sides of those pi values. So the claim that no tile can contain both L and R squares is false. Indeed, we can have a rectangle that lies entirely between two pi columns, covering rows whose pi values are either all above or all below the interval. For those rows, the rectangle is either left or right relative to each row's uncovered square. So the rectangle can contain squares from L (for rows where pi(i) > interval) and squares from R (for rows where pi(i) < interval). So the claim is false.\n\nThus the solution's lower bound argument is invalid. However, the solution uses this claim to separate the tilings of L and R. Without that claim, the lower bound may be lower. So the answer 4048 might not be minimal; perhaps we can achieve fewer tiles by using crossing rectangles that cover both sides.\n\nWe need to determine if the answer is indeed minimal. Let's think about the problem more generally. The problem is: we have an n x n board, need to cover all squares except a permutation matrix (n uncovered squares). Tiles are axis-aligned rectangles covering unit squares, no overlaps. We want to minimize number of rectangles.\n\nThis is reminiscent of covering a board with rectangles such that each row and column has exactly one uncovered cell. Equivalent to covering the complement of a permutation matrix. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover the complement of a permutation matrix is known to be n + (n - 1) = 2n - 1? Actually there is known result: The complement of a permutation matrix (i.e., the board minus the permutation) can be partitioned into n-1 \"staircase\" shapes? Not sure.\n\nLet's analyze the problem more systematically.\n\nWe have n uncovered squares (one per row and column). The uncovered squares form a permutation pi. The covered squares are all other squares. We need to partition the covered squares into axis-aligned rectangles (tiles). Each tile is a rectangle of unit squares, no overlaps, covering all covered squares.\n\nGoal: minimize number of rectangles.\n\nThis is essentially a covering problem: partition the complement of a permutation matrix into rectangles. Equivalent to finding a rectangle partition of the board with holes at the permutation positions.\n\nWe can think of the board as n x n grid. The uncovered squares are like holes. We need to tile the rest. The minimal number of rectangles needed to tile a board with holes is related to the number of connected components of the complement region? Not exactly.\n\nWe can think of each rectangle as a maximal rectangle that does not contain any uncovered square. The uncovered squares create constraints.\n\nWe can think of the complement as a bipartite graph: rows and columns, edges representing covered squares. The rectangles correspond to complete bipartite subgraphs? Actually a rectangle covering rows I and columns J corresponds to covering all squares (i,j) for i in I, j in J. This rectangle is allowed only if none of the squares (i,j) for i in I, j in J is uncovered (i.e., (i, pi(i)) not in J for any i in I). So the rectangle must avoid the uncovered squares: for each row i in I, pi(i) must not be in J. So J must be a subset of columns that avoids the pi(i) for each i in I. Equivalent to: For each i in I, either pi(i) < min(J) or pi(i) > max(J). So pi(i) must be either all less than all columns in J or all greater than all columns in J. So the rectangle's column interval must be either entirely to the left of pi(i) for each i in I, or entirely to the right of pi(i) for each i in I. But for different rows i in I, the condition could be different: some rows may have pi(i) < min(J) (so rectangle is to the right of those uncovered squares), and some rows may have pi(i) > max(J) (so rectangle is to the left of those uncovered squares). So the rectangle can be on the left side for some rows and on the right side for others, as long as the interval J does not intersect any pi(i) for i in I. So J must be a set of columns that does not contain any pi(i) for i in I. Since J is a contiguous interval (rectangle), it must be a contiguous set of columns that avoids all pi(i) for i in I. So the condition is that the interval J must lie in a gap of the set {pi(i) | i in I}. Since pi(i) are distinct, there are n columns, and the set of pi(i) for rows in I is a subset of columns. The complement of that set includes intervals (gaps) where we can place J. So any rectangle corresponds to selecting a set of rows I and a column interval J that lies entirely in a gap of the pi-values for those rows.\n\nThus the problem reduces to partitioning the set of all rows and columns into rectangles that avoid pi-values.\n\nGoal: minimize number of rectangles covering all squares except the permutation points.\n\nWe can think of each rectangle as covering a product I x J, where I is a set of rows, J is a contiguous interval of columns that does not intersect pi(I). The uncovered squares are points (i, pi(i)). So we need to cover all other squares.\n\nWe can think of the complement as a bipartite graph with edges between rows and columns except the perfect matching defined by pi. We need to partition the edge set into complete bipartite subgraphs (I x J) where J is an interval.\n\nBut the intervals constraint is important: J must be contiguous.\n\nThus the problem is to cover the complement of a perfect matching in a complete bipartite graph with intervals.\n\nNow, the minimal number of rectangles might be related to the number of \"breakpoints\" in the permutation.\n\nThe solution's lower bound uses the fact that each row with left squares must have a distinct tile covering its rightmost left square, and each row with right squares must have a distinct tile covering its leftmost right square. However, that argument assumes that each tile can only cover squares from L or R exclusively, which is false. But perhaps the argument can be salvaged: Even if a tile can cover squares from both L and R, perhaps the rightmost left squares and leftmost right squares cannot be covered by the same tile, because they are on opposite sides of the rectangle interval J. Let's examine.\n\nSuppose we have a rectangle I x J that contains both a left square from row i (i.e., column j_L < pi(i)) and a right square from row k (i.e., column j_R > pi(k)). Then J must contain both j_L and j_R. Since J is a contiguous interval, it must contain all columns between j_L and j_R. Since pi(i) > j_L (by definition) and pi(k) < j_R, it's possible that pi(i) and pi(k) lie outside J, but J includes columns between them. For the rectangle to avoid uncovered squares, we need that for each row r in I, pi(r) not in J. So for row i, pi(i) must not be in J. Since J includes columns up to j_R, and pi(i) > j_L, but we need pi(i) not in J. So pi(i) must be > max(J) = j_R, i.e., pi(i) > j_R. Similarly, for row k, pi(k) must be < min(J) = j_L. So pi(k) < j_L. So for a rectangle to contain both left and right squares, the uncovered columns for those rows must be outside the interval J on opposite sides: pi(i) > max(J) and pi(k) < min(J). So indeed the rectangle's column interval must be a gap between pi(k) and pi(i). So the rectangle can contain left squares for rows whose pi(i) > max(J) (i.e., rows where uncovered column is to the right of the interval) and right squares for rows whose pi(i) < min(J) (uncovered column to left). So the rectangle can contain both left and right squares, but the uncovered columns for those rows must be on opposite sides of the interval.\n\nNow, consider the rightmost left square for a row i: that is column pi(i)-1 (if pi(i)>1). This column is the immediate left neighbor of the uncovered square. For a rectangle to contain that square, the interval J must include column pi(i)-1. For the rectangle to avoid covering the uncovered square (i, pi(i)), we need pi(i) not in J. Since J includes pi(i)-1, and J is contiguous, the only way to exclude pi(i) is to have max(J) = pi(i)-1 (i.e., J ends at pi(i)-1). So J must be an interval that ends at pi(i)-1. Similarly, for a rectangle to contain the leftmost right square for a row i (i.e., column pi(i)+1), the interval J must start at pi(i)+1.\n\nThus the rightmost left squares of different rows have distinct columns (since pi(i)-1 values are distinct). If we try to cover two such squares with a single rectangle, the rectangle's column interval must include both columns pi(i)-1 and pi(k)-1. Since the interval is contiguous, it must include all columns between them. If pi(i)-1 < pi(k)-1, then the interval includes columns up to pi(k)-1. To avoid covering uncovered squares, we need that for each row r in the rectangle, pi(r) not be in J. For rows i and k, pi(i) > pi(i)-1 = max(J) maybe? Actually if the interval includes pi(k)-1, and pi(k)-1 is the max column of interval (if pi(k)-1 is the max), then max(J) = pi(k)-1. To avoid covering (k, pi(k)), we need pi(k) > max(J) = pi(k)-1, which is true (since pi(k) = pi(k)-1 + 1). But pi(k) = pi(k)-1 + 1 = max(J)+1, which is not in J because J ends at pi(k)-1. So that's okay. For row i, pi(i) might be less than max(J) if pi(i) < pi(k). If pi(i) < pi(k), then pi(i) <= pi(k)-1 = max(J). Since pi(i) is distinct from pi(k)-1, pi(i) could be less than max(J). Then pi(i) would be inside J, which is not allowed. So we cannot have pi(i) < pi(k). So to cover both rightmost left squares in one rectangle, we need pi(i) > pi(k). That is, the row with larger pi(i) must be the one with larger column. So the rightmost left squares are arranged in decreasing order of pi(i). But the pi(i)-1 values are distinct, but they could be arranged in any order across rows. So can we group them into rectangles that each contain a set of rows where pi(i) values are all greater than any column in J? Let's think.\n\nSuppose we have rows i1,i2,...,im, with pi(i1) > pi(i2) > ... > pi(im). Then the rightmost left squares are at columns pi(i1)-1 > pi(i2)-1 > ... > pi(im)-1. If we take rectangle with rows {i1,...,im} and columns from pi(im)-1 to pi(i1)-1 (i.e., the interval covering all those columns), then for each row i_j, pi(i_j) is > pi(i_j)-1 = column of rightmost left square for that row. But pi(i_j) may be inside the interval for some rows. For row i1 (largest pi), pi(i1) > pi(i1)-1 = max column, so pi(i1) > max(J) = pi(i1)-1, so pi(i1) not in J. For row i2, pi(i2) is less than pi(i1) but greater than pi(i2)-1 = column for i2. Since J includes columns from pi(im)-1 up to pi(i1)-1, pi(i2) may be inside J if pi(i2) > pi(im)-1. Actually pi(i2) > pi(i2)-1 = column for i2, which is in J. Since J includes all columns up to pi(i1)-1, pi(i2) is likely less than pi(i1)-1, but greater than pi(im)-1. So pi(i2) is inside J. That would be prohibited. So we cannot group multiple rightmost left squares into a single rectangle unless the pi values are arranged such that all pi(i) are greater than max(J). That would require that all pi(i) are greater than the largest column in J. But the largest column in J is the largest of the pi(i)-1 values. Since pi(i) = (pi(i)-1)+1, the largest pi(i) is one more than the largest column. So only the row with the largest pi(i) can be in that rectangle; any other row will have pi(i) <= max(J) (since pi(i) <= pi(i1) - something). Actually if we have two rows i and k with pi(i) > pi(k). Then pi(k) <= pi(i)-1? Not necessarily. For example, pi(i)=10, pi(k)=9. Then pi(i)-1=9, pi(k)-1=8. The interval from 8 to 9 includes column 9, which is pi(i). So pi(i) is inside J, not allowed. So indeed only one row can be in a rectangle covering its rightmost left square. So each rightmost left square must be in a separate tile. So the lower bound of n-1 for L holds even without the claim that tiles cannot mix L and R. Actually the argument that each row with left squares forces a distinct tile to cover its rightmost left square does not rely on the claim that tiles cannot mix L and R. It only uses Lemma 1 that no rectangle can contain two rightmost left squares of different rows. That lemma is proven correctly (the proof considered same row case? Actually Lemma 1 considered two rightmost left squares (i, pi(i)-1) and (k, pi(k)-1) in different rows. The proof assumed wlog pi(i)-1 < pi(k)-1, then argued rectangle must contain (i, pi(i)). That proof is correct for any rectangle containing both squares, regardless of whether rectangle also contains other squares. It didn't rely on the claim that tiles cannot mix L and R. So Lemma 1 is valid.\n\nThus the lower bound for L is valid: each row with left squares must have a distinct tile covering its rightmost left square. So at least n-1 tiles are needed to cover L. Similarly for R, at least n-1 tiles needed. However, could a single tile cover both a rightmost left square of some row and a leftmost right square of another row? Possibly, but that would be a tile that contains squares from both L and R. The lower bound argument counts tiles needed for L and R separately, assuming they are disjoint sets of tiles. But if a tile can cover both a rightmost left square and a leftmost right square, then the total number of tiles needed could be less than sum of separate lower bounds, because one tile could serve both purposes. However, the lower bound argument for L uses the fact that each tile covering L must contain at most one rightmost left square. It does not forbid a tile covering also some right squares. So the lower bound for L says at least n-1 tiles that each contain a rightmost left square. Similarly, at least n-1 tiles that each contain a leftmost right square. But these tiles could be the same tile, i.e., a tile could contain both a rightmost left square of row i and a leftmost right square of row k. If that happens, then that tile would count for both lower bounds, reducing total count. The solution's argument that total tiles >= (n-1)+(n-1) implicitly assumes that the sets of tiles covering L and covering R are disjoint. They argued that because each tile lies wholly in L or wholly in R, the sets are disjoint. But we have shown that claim is false. So the lower bound of 2n-2 may not hold. However, we need to examine if there is any other argument that prevents a tile from covering both a rightmost left square and a leftmost right square. Let's examine.\n\nSuppose we have a tile that includes a rightmost left square (i, pi(i)-1) and a leftmost right square (k, pi(k)+1). For the tile to include (i, pi(i)-1), the column interval J must include pi(i)-1. To avoid covering (i, pi(i)), we need pi(i) not in J. Since pi(i) = pi(i)-1 + 1, J must end at pi(i)-1 (i.e., max(J) = pi(i)-1). So J is an interval whose maximum column is pi(i)-1. Similarly, to include (k, pi(k)+1) and avoid (k, pi(k)), we need J's minimum column = pi(k)+1 (i.e., min(J) = pi(k)+1). So J must start at pi(k)+1. Therefore, for a rectangle to contain both squares, we need pi(k)+1 <= pi(i)-1, i.e., pi(k)+2 <= pi(i). So pi(i) - pi(k) >= 2. So there is a gap of at least one column between pi(k) and pi(i). In that case, we can set J = [pi(k)+1, pi(i)-1] (inclusive). This interval does not contain pi(k) or pi(i). So the rectangle can avoid both uncovered squares. So indeed a rectangle can contain both a rightmost left square of row i and a leftmost right square of row k, provided pi(i) > pi(k)+1. So such a tile can serve both purposes.\n\nThus the lower bound 2n-2 is not valid. The minimal number of tiles could be less than 2n-2. Let's try to find a better lower bound or find a construction with fewer tiles.\n\nWe need to find the minimal number of rectangles to cover complement of a permutation matrix. This is known as the \"rectangle covering number\" of the complement of a permutation matrix. There might be known results: The rectangle covering number of the complement of a permutation matrix is n + (c-1) where c is the number of cycles in the permutation? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, we can think of the problem as: we have a permutation pi. We need to cover all squares (i,j) with j != pi(i). This is like covering all entries of an n x n matrix except the diagonal of a permutation. The minimal number of combinatorial rectangles (axis-aligned) needed to cover all off-diagonal entries is known as the \"rectangle covering number\" of the complement of a permutation matrix. This is related to the \"boolean rank\" of the matrix (the matrix with zeros on the permutation and ones elsewhere). The boolean rank of the complement of a permutation matrix is known to be at least log2(n) and at most something like n? Actually the boolean rank (or rectangle covering number) of the complement of a permutation matrix is known to be n (since each row has n-1 ones, you could cover each row with one rectangle that includes all columns except the uncovered column, but that rectangle would include the uncovered column for some rows? Wait, a rectangle covering a row i must be contiguous columns that avoid pi(i). So you could cover each row i with a rectangle that covers all columns except pi(i). But that rectangle would be two intervals: columns 1..pi(i)-1 and pi(i)+1..n. That's not a single contiguous interval, so you need two rectangles per row (one left side, one right side). So naive covering uses 2n rectangles (n left side intervals, n right side intervals). But we can combine rows that have the same side intervals.\n\nThe solution's construction uses n-1 vertical rectangles covering left side (for each column j from 1 to n-1, covering rows j+1..n). That's essentially grouping left squares by columns: each column j left side squares are those rows i > j where pi(i) > j? Actually for identity permutation, left side squares are those with i > j (since pi(i)=i). So each column j has left squares in rows i > j. So vertical rectangle covering column j and rows j+1..n covers all left squares in column j. That's optimal for identity permutation.\n\nSimilarly, horizontal rectangles cover right side squares.\n\nBut for a general permutation, we might be able to combine left and right squares across rows into fewer rectangles.\n\nThe lower bound argument using rightmost left squares and leftmost right squares yields at least n-1 tiles for each side, but they could be overlapped in a single tile. However, each tile can contain at most one rightmost left square (by Lemma 1) and at most one leftmost right square (by analogous argument). So each tile can cover at most one rightmost left square and at most one leftmost right square. So each tile can cover at most two of those special squares (one from each side). Since there are n-1 rightmost left squares and n-1 leftmost right squares, the total number of tiles needed is at least ceil((2n-2)/2) = n-1? Actually each tile can cover at most one of each type, so each tile can cover at most 2 special squares. So the minimum number of tiles needed is at least max(n-1, n-1) = n-1? Wait, we need to cover all rightmost left squares and all leftmost right squares. Each tile can cover at most one rightmost left square and at most one leftmost right square. So each tile can cover at most 2 special squares, but we need to cover 2(n-1) special squares. So the lower bound is at least ceil(2(n-1)/2) = n-1. So we have a lower bound of n-1. But we also need to cover other squares besides