es \\(j\\le i\\), a contradiction. ∎  \n   - Because \\(|S|=n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(U\\). By symmetry the same holds for \\(L\\). Hence any partition of the whole board requires at least \\(2(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n5. **Conclusion:** The construction shows \\(2n-2\\) tiles are achievable, and the lower‑bound argument shows no fewer tiles can suffice. For \\(n=2025\\) this yields \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe have an \\(n\\times n\\) grid of unit squares.  \nMatilda must place axis‑parallel rectangular tiles whose sides lie on grid lines, no two tiles may overlap, and each row and each column must contain **exactly one** unit square that is not covered by any tile.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to the main diagonal\n\nLet \\(\\sigma\\) be the permutation that records, for each row \\(i\\), the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) of the uncovered square in that row.  \nPermuting the rows according to the identity map and the columns according to \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\) merely relabels the grid; a tiling for the original permutation is transformed into a tiling for the identity permutation and vice‑versa.  \nHence we may assume without loss of generality that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nthe main diagonal of the board.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Geometry of admissible rectangles\n\nA rectangle is determined by a contiguous set of rows \\([a,b]\\) and a contiguous set of columns \\([c,d]\\) (with \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\)).  \nThe rectangle contains the diagonal square \\((k,k)\\) iff \\(k\\in[a,b]\\cap[c,d]\\).  \nSince no diagonal square may be covered, we must have  \n\n\\[\n[a,b]\\cap[c,d]=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus every admissible rectangle lies **entirely** either\n\n* **above** the diagonal: \\(\\displaystyle b<c\\) (all its rows are numbered smaller than all its columns), or  \n* **below** the diagonal: \\(\\displaystyle d<a\\) (all its columns are numbered smaller than all its rows).\n\nConsequently the set of covered squares splits into two independent parts:\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}\\qquad\\text{(strictly upper‑triangular)},\n\\]\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}\\qquad\\text{(strictly lower‑triangular)}.\n\\]\n\nA rectangle belongs to exactly one of these two families.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\qquad\nV_i=\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{i\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n* \\(H_i\\) is a horizontal rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\); it lies in the upper‑triangular region because every column in \\(\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) exceeds the row index \\(i\\).  \n* \\(V_i\\) is a vertical rectangle of size \\((n-i)\\times 1\\); it lies in the lower‑triangular region because every row in \\(\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) exceeds the column index \\(i\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{V_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint:\n\n* Two distinct horizontal rectangles occupy distinct rows, so they cannot intersect.  \n* Two distinct vertical rectangles occupy distinct columns, so they cannot intersect.  \n* A horizontal rectangle \\(H_i\\) uses row \\(i\\) and columns \\(>i\\); a vertical rectangle \\(V_j\\) uses column \\(j\\) and rows \\(>j\\).  \n  If they intersect, we would need a square \\((i,j)\\) with simultaneously \\(j>i\\) (from \\(H_i\\)) and \\(i>j\\) (from \\(V_j\\)), impossible.\n\nEvery square of \\(U\\) is of the form \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\); it belongs to the unique horizontal rectangle \\(H_i\\).  \nEvery square of \\(L\\) is of the form \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\); it belongs to the unique vertical