umn \\(i\\) below the diagonal.  \n   The \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{H_i\\}\\cup\\{V_i\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, avoid all diagonal cells, and together cover every other unit square.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is tight; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe work with the \\(n\\times n\\) grid of unit squares whose rows are indexed by\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and whose columns are indexed by\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to the diagonal configuration\n\nSuppose a set \\(U\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) of uncovered\nsquares satisfies the condition \u201cexactly one uncovered square in each row and\neach column\u201d.  Then there is a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such\nthat  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nApply the row permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the identity) and the column\npermutation \\(\\tau(j)=\\pi^{-1}(j)\\).  After permuting rows by \\(\\sigma\\) and\ncolumns by \\(\\tau\\) the set \\(U\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\nU'=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the main diagonal.  Any tiling of the original board by axis\u2011aligned\nrectangles is carried by the same permutations to a tiling of the transformed\nboard with the same number of rectangles, because a rectangle is defined only\nby the sets of its rows and columns.  Consequently, the minimum number of\ntiles needed for an arbitrary permutation \\(\\pi\\) equals the minimum number needed\nwhen the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal cells.  Hence it suffices\nto solve the problem for the diagonal configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Geometry of a rectangle avoiding the diagonal\n\nLet \\(R\\) be a rectangle whose interior consists solely of unit squares and\nwhich contains no diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(r,c)\\mid a\\le r\\le b,\\;c\\le d\\le d\\}\n\\]\n\nfor some integers \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nIf there exist indices \\(r\\) and \\(c\\) with \\(r<c\\) (a cell above the diagonal)\nand also indices \\(r'\\) and \\(c'\\) with \\(r'>c'\\) (a cell below the diagonal)\ninside \\(R\\), then the intervals \\([a,b]\\) and \\([c,d]\\) intersect; let\n\\(k\\) be any integer belonging to the intersection.  The cell \\((k,k)\\) would\nbelong to \\(R\\), contradicting the hypothesis that \\(R\\) avoids the diagonal.\nTherefore **every rectangle avoiding the diagonal lies entirely either in the\nupper triangular region**  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\},\n\\]\n\nor in the lower triangular region  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Lower bound for the upper triangular region\n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nS_U=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe left\u2011most cell of each row of \\(U\\).  The cells of \\(S_U\\) are pairwise\nnon\u2011adjacent; they form a \u201cstaircase\u201d.\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim.}\\quad\\text{A rectangle contained in }U\\text{ can contain at most\none element of }S_U.\n\\]\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(R\\subseteq U\\) contains two distinct cells\n\\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((k,k+1)\\) with \\(i<k\\).  Then the row interval of \\(R\\)\ncontains \\(i\\) and \\(k\\), and the column interval contains \\(i+1\\) and\n\\(k+1\\).  Consequently the cell \\((k,i+1)\\) belongs to \\(R\\) (its row is in the\ninterval and its column is in the interval).  Since \\(k>i+1\\), the cell\n\\((k,i+1)\\) lies **below** the diagonal, i.e. it is not an element of \\(U\\).\nThus \\(R\\) would contain a cell outside \\(U\\), contradicting the assumption\n\\(R\\subseteq U\\).  \u220e\n\nBecause \\(|S_U|=n-1\\) and each rectangle of a tiling of \\(U\\) can cover at most\none element of \\(S_U\\), any tiling of \\(U\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Lower bound for the lower triangular region\n\nBy symmetry, the lower triangular region \\(L\\) contains the set  \n\n\\[\nS_L=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\},\n\\]\n\nand the same argument shows that a rectangle contained in \\(L\\) can contain at\nmost one element of \\(S_L\\).  Hence any tiling of \\(L\\) also requires at least\n\\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Overall lower bound\n\nSince a tiling of the whole board must cover both \\(U\\) and \\(L\\) and the two\nparts are disjoint, the total number of rectangles in any tiling is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Construction attaining the bound\n\nDefine, for each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\),\n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n\\qquad\nV_i=\\{(j,i)\\mid i<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*   \\(H_i\\) is a horizontal rectangle covering the whole part of row \\(i\\)\n    to the right of the diagonal.\n*   \\(V_i\\) is a vertical rectangle covering the whole part of column \\(i\\)\n    below the diagonal.\n\nThe families \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{V_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise\ndisjoint, they avoid all diagonal cells, and their union equals\n\\(U\\cup L\\), i.e. all squares of the board except the diagonal.  The total\nnumber of rectangles is  \n\n\\[\n| \\{H_i\\}|+|\\{V_i\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7. Conclusion\n\nFrom (3) we have a lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) rectangles for any admissible\ntiling, and from (4) we have a construction achieving this bound.\nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is precisely \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]"
