                            "text": "We need to verify the provided solution according to the hierarchical decision tree and the extra constraints about construction claims.\n\nThe problem: We have a 2025x2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda wants to place rectangular tiles (axis-aligned) covering unit squares, each tile's sides lie on grid lines, each unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles needed so that each row and each column has exactly one unit square not covered (i.e., each row and each column has exactly one uncovered square). So we need to find the minimum number of tiles needed to achieve that condition.\n\nThe solution: They set n=2025. They define uncovered squares as a permutation: one uncovered per row and column, so the uncovered squares form a set U = {(i, π(i))}. Then they define sets A and B as points immediately to the right (or below) of uncovered squares (except when at the border). They argue that each tile can contain at most one point from A and at most one point from B, giving lower bound t ≥ |A|+|B| ≥ 2n-2. Then they construct a tiling achieving 2n-2 tiles: choose diagonal permutation π(i)=i, uncovered squares are diagonal cells. Then for each i=1,...,n-1 define horizontal strip H_i = {(i,j) | i<j ≤ n} (i.e., row i, columns to the right of diagonal) and vertical strip V_i = {(j,i) | i<j ≤ n} (i.e., column i, rows below diagonal). These are 2(n-1) rectangles covering all off-diagonal cells. They argue they are disjoint and avoid uncovered squares. So they achieve 2n-2 tiles. So the minimum is 2n-2 = 4048.\n\nWe need to check answer correctness: final answer is 4048. Is that correct? Let's think about the problem: The solution seems plausible. Let's verify the lower bound and construction.\n\nWe need to ensure the lower bound is correct: they claim each tile can contain at most one point of A and at most one point of B. Then they sum |A|+|B| to get lower bound. But is that correct? Let's examine the reasoning: A = {(i, π(i)+1) | π(i) < n}. So these are squares to the right of each uncovered square, except those in the last column. Similarly B = {(π^{-1}(j)+1, j) | π^{-1}(j) < n} are squares below each uncovered square, except those in the last row. So there are at least n-1 points in each set. They claim each rectangle can contain at most one point of A (and at most one point of B). Then each tile can cover at most one point from A and at most one from B. So each tile can cover at most 2 points total? Actually they claim each tile can cover at most one element of A and at most one element of B, but they treat them as additive: total number of tiles t ≥ |A|+|B|. However, a tile could contain one point from A and one point from B simultaneously, so it could cover two points (one from each set). But they treat each tile as covering at most one from each set, but they sum the counts. The inequality t ≥ |A|+|B| is derived from each tile covering at most one from each set, but that doesn't guarantee that each tile covers exactly one from each set; some tiles might cover none, some might cover one from A and one from B. But the sum of tiles needed to cover all points in A and all points in B is at least max(|A|,|B|) if each tile can cover at most one from each set? Actually we need to think: Suppose we have t tiles, each tile can cover at most one point from A and at most one point from B. So each tile can cover at most 2 points total (one from each set). But we need to cover all points in A and B (since all points in A and B are covered squares). So we need enough tiles to cover all points in A and B. If each tile can cover at most one from A, then we need at least |A| tiles to cover all points in A, because each tile can cover at most one point from A. Similarly, we need at least |B| tiles to cover all points in B. However, the same tile could cover a point from A and a point from B simultaneously, thus we might be able to use fewer than |A|+|B| tiles. But the lower bound t ≥ max(|A|,|B|) is correct, not t ≥ |A|+|B|. However, they claim t ≥ |A|+|B|. Let's examine: If each tile can cover at most one point from A, then we need at least |A| tiles. That's a lower bound. Similarly, need at least |B| tiles. So we need at least max(|A|,|B|) tiles, but not necessarily sum. However, they claim t ≥ |A|+|B|. That is too strong unless they also argue that each tile can contain at most one point from A ∪ B (i.e., cannot contain both a point from A and a point from B). But they didn't prove that; they only proved each tile can contain at most one from A and at most one from B, but they didn't show that a tile cannot contain both a point from A and a point from B. Actually they might have implicitly used that each tile can contain at most one from A and at most one from B, and then they sum the counts to get t ≥ |A|+|B|. But the correct lower bound is t ≥ max(|A|,|B|) if the tile can cover both a point from A and a point from B. However, can a tile contain both a point from A and a point from B? Let's check: A points are to the right of uncovered squares, B points are below uncovered squares. Could a rectangle contain both a point from A and a point from B? Possibly yes. For example, consider uncovered squares at (i,i). Then A includes (i,i+1) for i=1,...,n-1 (right of diagonal). B includes (i+1,i) for i=1,...,n-1 (below diagonal). A rectangle that covers the region (i,i+1) and (i+1,i) would need to include both points (i,i+1) and (i+1,i). But those points are not in the same row or column; they are at positions (i,i+1) and (i+1,i). The rectangle covering both would be the rectangle with rows from i to i+1 and columns from i to i+1, i.e., a 2x2 block covering cells (i,i), (i,i+1), (i+1,i), (i+1,i+1). However, that rectangle would contain the uncovered square (i,i) (the diagonal) or (i+1,i+1) depending on which uncovered squares are present. Actually for diagonal permutation, uncovered squares are (i,i). The rectangle covering (i,i+1) and (i+1,i) would also contain (i,i) and (i+1,i+1) which are uncovered squares, which is not allowed. So that rectangle cannot be used. But maybe there are other permutations where a rectangle could contain both a point from A and a point from B without containing any uncovered squares. Let's think: Suppose uncovered squares are at positions (i,π(i)). A point in A is (i,π(i)+1). A point in B is (π^{-1}(j)+1, j). For a rectangle to contain both, we need a pair (i,π(i)+1) and (π^{-1}(j)+1, j) inside same rectangle. The rectangle must avoid uncovered squares. Could there be a rectangle that contains both a right neighbor of one uncovered square and a below neighbor of another uncovered square, but not containing any uncovered squares? Possibly if the two points are far apart and the rectangle's row interval and column interval avoid the uncovered squares. But we need to check if the rectangle can be arranged to avoid all uncovered squares while containing both points.\n\nConsider two uncovered squares (i,π(i)) and (k,π(k)). Suppose we want to include (i,π(i)+1) (right neighbor of first) and (π^{-1}(j)+1, j) (below neighbor of second). Let's denote the B point as (π^{-1}(j)+1, j). That B point is below uncovered square (π^{-1}(j), j). So the B point's row index is one more than the row of the uncovered square in column j.\n\nThus the rectangle containing both points must contain rows from min(i, π^{-1}(j)+1) to max(i, π^{-1}(j)+1) and columns from min(π(i)+1, j) to max(π(i)+1, j). The rectangle might contain uncovered squares (i,π(i)) or (π^{-1}(j), j) or others. Possibly it could avoid them if the intervals do not include those coordinates. But note that the rectangle includes the point (i,π(i)+1). The column interval includes π(i)+1. The row interval includes i. So if there is an uncovered square at (i,π(i)), that is at column π(i) which is one less than π(i)+1, so it's not necessarily inside the rectangle unless the column interval includes π(i). Since column interval is from min(π(i)+1, j) to max(π(i)+1, j). If j > π(i)+1, then column interval includes columns from π(i)+1 up to j, which does not include π(i). So (i,π(i)) is not inside. If j < π(i)+1, then column interval includes j up to π(i)+1, which includes π(i) only if π(i) is between j and π(i)+1 inclusive. Since π(i) = π(i)+1 - 1, if j ≤ π(i) ≤ π(i)+1, then π(i) is in the interval, but if j ≤ π(i) then π(i) is included. So if j ≤ π(i), then (i,π(i)) is inside rectangle. So to avoid that, we need j > π(i). Similarly, we need to avoid the uncovered square (π^{-1}(j), j). The row interval includes π^{-1}(j)+1, so it includes row π^{-1}(j) only if i ≤ π^{-1}(j) ≤ π^{-1}(j)+1. That is, if i ≤ π^{-1}(j) ≤ π^{-1}(j)+1, i.e., i ≤ π^{-1}(j). So if i ≤ π^{-1}(j), then row π^{-1}(j) is inside interval, so (π^{-1}(j), j) is inside rectangle. So to avoid that, we need i > π^{-1}(j). So the condition to avoid both uncovered squares is: j > π(i) and i > π^{-1}(j). That is, j > π(i) and i > π^{-1}(j). This is reminiscent of the condition that the pair (i,j) is \"above and to the right\" of the permutation graph. Indeed, the condition i > π^{-1}(j) is equivalent to π(i) > j? Wait, let's rewrite: π(i) is the column of the uncovered square in row i. π^{-1}(j) is the row of the uncovered square in column j. So the condition i > π^{-1}(j) means row i is below the row of the uncovered square in column j. And j > π(i) means column j is to the right of the column of the uncovered square in row i. So the rectangle containing (i,π(i)+1) and (π^{-1}(j)+1,j) will avoid uncovered squares if the uncovered squares are \"southwest\" of each other? Actually we need both conditions: j > π(i) and i > π^{-1}(j). This is exactly the condition that the point (i,j) lies strictly southeast of the permutation point (i,π(i)) and also strictly southeast of (π^{-1}(j),j). That is, the rectangle's lower right corner (i,j) is strictly southeast of both uncovered squares. In that case, the rectangle from rows i to π^{-1}(j)+1 and columns π(i)+1 to j will not contain any uncovered squares because the uncovered squares are above or left of the rectangle.\n\nBut does such a rectangle exist? Yes, if there are two uncovered squares such that i > π^{-1}(j) and j > π(i). This is possible for many permutations. For example, consider π = identity (i,i). Then π(i) = i, π^{-1}(j) = j. The condition i > π^{-1}(j) becomes i > j, and j > π(i) becomes j > i. These cannot both hold simultaneously. So for identity permutation, no rectangle can contain both a right neighbor and a below neighbor without containing an uncovered square. So the lower bound is tight for identity.\n\nBut for other permutations, maybe we can have a rectangle that contains both a point from A and a point from B. For example, consider π = reverse permutation: π(i) = n+1-i. Then π(i) = n+1-i, π^{-1}(j) = n+1-j. The condition i > π^{-1}(j) becomes i > n+1-j, i + j > n+1. And j > π(i) becomes j > n+1-i, i + j > n+1. So both conditions reduce to i + j > n+1. So any pair (i,j) with i + j > n+1 would satisfy both. So there are many such pairs. So for reverse permutation, there exist rectangles that contain both a point from A and a point from B. Let's test with n=5 for simplicity: π = [5,4,3,2,1]. Then A includes points (i,π(i)+1) for π(i) < n. For i=1, π(1)=5, which is n, so no A point. For i=2, π(2)=4, so A includes (2,5). For i=3, π(3)=3, A includes (3,4). For i=4, π(4)=2, A includes (4,3). For i=5, π(5)=1, A includes (5,2). B includes points (π^{-1}(j)+1, j) for π^{-1}(j) < n. Since π^{-1}(j) = n+1-j, for j=1, π^{-1}(1)=5, no B point. For j=2, π^{-1}(2)=4, B includes (5,2). For j=3, π^{-1}(3)=3, B includes (4,3). For j=4, π^{-1}(4)=2, B includes (3,4). For j=5, π^{-1}(5)=1, B includes (2,5). So A = {(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2)} and B = {(5,2),(4,3),(3,4),(2,5)}. So A and B are the same set, each point appears in both sets. In this case, each point is both a right neighbor of one uncovered square and a below neighbor of another uncovered square. So each tile can contain at most one point from A (and at most one from B). But since A and B are the same set, each tile can contain at most one point total (since if it contains a point, it's both an A and B point). So the bound t ≥ |A|+|B| = 2|A| = 2*(n-1) = 2n-2 would be too high because each tile can only cover one point, but the bound says we need at least 2n-2 tiles. However, we have |A| = n-1 = 4 for n=5, so bound says t ≥ 8. But we can cover all points with 4 tiles? Let's see. For reverse permutation, uncovered squares are (i, n+1-i). The off-diagonal squares are all other squares. Could we tile them with 4 tiles? Possibly we could use a single tile covering many squares. Let's try to see if we can tile with fewer than 2n-2. The solution's construction uses diagonal permutation and yields exactly 2n-2 tiles. But maybe there is a better tiling using a different permutation that yields fewer tiles. The lower bound they derived may not be tight for all permutations. But they claim it's a lower bound for any tiling, regardless of permutation. However, their lower bound may be flawed because they double count points that could be covered by the same tile. They claim each tile can cover at most one point from A and at most one point from B, but they then sum the counts, which is not valid because a tile could cover one point from each set. So the lower bound should be t ≥ max(|A|,|B|) ≥ n-1, which is trivial. But they claim t ≥ 2n-2, which is stronger. So the lower bound may be invalid.\n\nBut we need to check if the solution's lower bound is indeed valid. Let's examine the lemma: \"Let R be a rectangle that contains no point of U. Then |R∩A| ≤ 1 and |R∩B| ≤ 1.\" They prove that a rectangle cannot contain two points of A. Similarly for B. So each rectangle can contain at most one point from A and at most one point from B. But they didn't prove that a rectangle cannot contain both a point from A and a point from B. However, they might have implicitly used that each tile can contain at most one point from A∪B. But they didn't prove that. The lemma only says at most one from each set. So a rectangle could contain one from each set, i.e., up to two points total. So the lower bound t ≥ |A|+|B| is not justified.\n\nThus the reasoning is flawed. However, we need to check if the solution's lower bound is indeed correct or if there is a flaw. The solution's lower bound is crucial for establishing minimal number of tiles. If the lower bound is incorrect, then the answer may not be correct. However, the answer may still be correct if the lower bound is correct, but we suspect it's not. Let's verify with a small example to see if we can tile a 3x3 grid with fewer than 2n-2 = 4 tiles. For n=3, we need each row and column to have exactly one uncovered square. So we need 3 uncovered squares. The total squares are 9, so we need to cover 6 squares with tiles. Tiles are axis-aligned rectangles covering unit squares, disjoint, not covering uncovered squares. The minimal number of tiles? Let's try to find a tiling with 3 tiles maybe. For n=3, 2n-2 = 4. Could we do with 3? Let's try to find a permutation and tiling.\n\nPermutation: identity (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) uncovered. Then we need to cover off-diagonal squares: (1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2). The solution's construction uses H1 = row1 columns 2-3 (covers (1,2),(1,3)), H2 = row2 columns 3 (covers (2,3)), V1 = column1 rows 2-3 (covers (2,1),(3,1)), V2 = column2 rows 3 (covers (3,2)). That's 4 tiles: H1, H2, V1, V2. That's indeed 4 = 2n-2. Could we tile with 3 tiles? Suppose we try a tile covering (1,2),(1,3),(2,3) as a shape? That's not a rectangle (L shape). Tiles must be rectangles. Could we have a rectangle covering (1,2),(1,3),(2,2),(2,3)? That includes (2,2) which is uncovered, not allowed. Could we have a rectangle covering (1,2),(2,1),(2,2)? No, includes uncovered. Could we have a rectangle covering (1,2),(2,1) as a 2x2 block? That includes (1,1) and (2,2) uncovered. So not allowed. Could we have a rectangle covering (1,2),(2,2) but (2,2) is uncovered. So not allowed. So maybe 4 is minimal for n=3. Let's test n=4: 2n-2 = 6. Could we tile with fewer? Let's try to find a permutation that yields a tiling with fewer than 6. For n=4, we need 4 uncovered squares, covering 12 squares. Could we tile with 5 rectangles? Possibly. Let's try to find a tiling manually. The diagonal construction yields 6 tiles: H1 (row1 columns 2-4), H2 (row2 columns 3-4), H3 (row3 column 4), V1 (col1 rows 2-4), V2 (col2 rows 3-4), V3 (col3 row4). That's 6.\n\nBut maybe we can use larger rectangles that cover more squares. For example, consider permutation (1,2),(2,1),(3,4),(4,3). Uncovered squares: (1,2),(2,1),(3,4),(4,3). Let's try to tile the rest. The uncovered squares are arranged in a pattern. The sets A and B: A includes right neighbors: (1,3) (since π(1)=2 <4), (2,2) (π(2)=1 <4), (3,5?) but n=4 so π(3)=4 => no A for i=3, (4,4?) π(4)=3 => A includes (4,4). B includes below neighbors: (2,2) (since π^{-1}(2)=1 <4), (1,1) (π^{-1}(1)=2 <4), (4,4) (π^{-1}(4)=3 <4), (3,3) (π^{-1}(3)=4? Actually π^{-1}(3)=4, which is n, so no B for j=3). So A = {(1,3),(2,2),(4,4)}. B = {(2,2),(1,1),(4,4)}. So A∪B = {(1,3),(2,2),(1,1),(4,4)}. So there are 4 points that must be covered. Each tile can cover at most one from A and at most one from B, but could cover both (2,2) is both in A and B, same for (4,4). So we need at least 2 tiles? Actually we need to cover at least 4 points, but each tile can cover at most 2 points (one from each set). So lower bound t ≥ ceil(|A|+|B|)/2? Actually we need to cover each point in A and each point in B. But if a point belongs to both sets, covering it covers both. So we need to cover all points in A∪B, but the constraints are more subtle. However, the lower bound t ≥ max(|A|,|B|) = 3. So we need at least 3 tiles. Could we tile with 3? Possibly.\n\nBut the solution's lower bound says t ≥ 2n-2 = 6. That seems too high for n=4 maybe. Let's try to find a tiling with 5 or fewer for n=4. Let's attempt to tile with 5 rectangles. Let's try to use large rectangles that avoid uncovered squares.\n\nWe need to cover all squares except the 4 uncovered squares. The uncovered squares are at positions (1,2),(2,1),(3,4),(4,3). Let's draw a 4x4 grid:\n\nRows 1 to 4, columns 1 to 4.\n\nUncovered squares:\n- (1,2)\n- (2,1)\n- (3,4)\n- (4,3)\n\nWe need to cover all other squares: total squares = 16, uncovered = 4, so need to cover 12 squares.\n\nWe can try to use rectangles that avoid uncovered squares. Let's see possible large rectangles:\n\n- The rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 3-4: includes squares (1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4). Does it contain any uncovered? No, uncovered squares are at (1,2),(2,1),(3,4),(4,3). So this rectangle is safe. That's one tile covering 4 squares.\n\n- The rectangle covering rows 3-4, columns 1-2: includes (3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2). Does it contain any uncovered? Uncovered squares at (3,4),(4,3) are not in this rectangle. So safe. That's second tile covering 4 squares.\n\nNow we have covered 8 squares, leaving 4 squares uncovered (the uncovered squares themselves) plus the squares (1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4) are covered, (3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2) covered. The remaining squares are:\n\n- Row 1: column 1 (1,1) not covered yet.\n- Row 1: column 2 is uncovered (1,2).\n- Row 2: column 2 (2,2) not covered.\n- Row 2: column 1 is uncovered (2,1).\n- Row 3: column 3 (3,3) not covered.\n- Row 3: column 4 is uncovered (3,4).\n- Row 4: column 3 is uncovered (4,3).\n- Row 4: column 4 (4,4) not covered.\n\nSo we have 4 squares that are not uncovered but not covered yet: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(4,4). These are the diagonal squares. They are not uncovered squares (the uncovered squares are off-diagonal). So we need to cover these four squares. They are isolated: they are not adjacent in rows/columns? Actually they are each in distinct rows and columns, but we could possibly cover them with a rectangle? For example, rectangle covering rows 1-4, columns 1-4 includes uncovered squares, not allowed. Could we cover (1,1) and (2,2) with a rectangle? That rectangle would be rows 1-2, columns 1-2, includes (1,2) and (2,1) which are uncovered squares. So not allowed. Could we cover (1,1) alone as a 1x1 tile? Yes, a tile can be a 1x1 rectangle covering a single unit square. Similarly for (2,2),(3,3),(4,4). So we need 4 more tiles, making total 6 tiles. So we have a tiling with 6 tiles: two large 2x2 rectangles covering the corners, and four 1x1 tiles covering the diagonal squares. That's 6 tiles, which matches the lower bound 2n-2 = 6. Could we do better? Could we combine some of those diagonal squares into a rectangle that avoids uncovered squares? Let's try to combine (1,1) and (2,2) with a rectangle that also includes (1,2) or (2,1) which are uncovered, so not allowed. Could we combine (3,3) and (4,4) similarly? That rectangle would include (3,4) or (4,3) uncovered. So not allowed. Could we combine (1,1) and (3,3) with a rectangle covering rows 1-3, columns 1-3? That includes (1,2),(2,1) uncovered. So not allowed. Could we combine (2,2) and (4,4) with rows 2-4, columns 2-4? That includes (3,4),(4,3) uncovered. So not allowed. Could we combine (1,1) and (4,4) with rows 1-4, columns 1-4? That includes all uncovered squares. So not allowed. So we cannot combine any two of those diagonal squares without including an uncovered square. So we need at least 4 tiles for those squares. So total tiles needed is at least 6. So for this permutation, 6 is minimal. So the lower bound 2n-2 holds for n=4 as well. However, we need to examine if there is any permutation that yields a tiling with fewer than 2n-2 tiles. The solution's lower bound is based on sets A and B each of size at least n-1, and each tile can contain at most one point from each set. But we argued that a tile could contain one from each set, so the lower bound could be as low as n-1. But maybe there is an additional argument that each tile can contain at most one point from A∪B, i.e., cannot contain both a point from A and a point from B. Let's examine the lemma again: they proved that a rectangle cannot contain two points of A. They also claim \"the same argument, applied to the transpose of the board, shows that |R∩B| ≤ 1\". But they didn't show that a rectangle cannot contain both a point from A and a point from B. However, maybe the geometry of A and B ensures that any rectangle containing a point from A cannot contain a point from B, because of the relative positions of these points. Let's examine the definitions:\n\n- A = {(i, π(i)+1) | π(i) < n}. So each point in A is directly to the right of an uncovered square (i, π(i)). So A points are at coordinates (i, π(i)+1). For each i, the column index is one more than the column of the uncovered square in that row.\n\n- B = {(π^{-1}(j)+1, j) | π^{-1}(j) < n}. So each point in B is directly below an uncovered square (π^{-1}(j), j). So B points are at coordinates (π^{-1}(j)+1, j). For each column j, the row index is one more than the row of the uncovered square in that column.\n\nThus A points are \"right neighbors\" of uncovered squares, B points are \"below neighbors\". In the grid, these points are arranged such that each A point is in the same row as its corresponding uncovered square, and each B point is in the same column as its corresponding uncovered square.\n\nNow, can a rectangle avoid uncovered squares and contain both a right neighbor of one uncovered square and a below neighbor of another uncovered square? Let's consider two uncovered squares (i, π(i)) and (k, π(k)). The A point from the first is (i, π(i)+1). The B point from the second is (π^{-1}(j)+1, j) where j = π(k). So B point from second is (k+1, π(k)). Wait, careful: B points are defined as (π^{-1}(j)+1, j). If we take j = π(k), then π^{-1}(j) = k. So B point is (k+1, π(k)). So the B point from uncovered square (k, π(k)) is (k+1, π(k)). So the A point from (i, π(i)) is (i, π(i)+1). So we have points (i, π(i)+1) and (k+1, π(k)). For a rectangle to contain both, we need row interval covering i and k+1, column interval covering π(i)+1 and π(k). The rectangle must avoid uncovered squares (i, π(i)) and (k, π(k)), as well as any other uncovered squares.\n\nNow, does the rectangle containing these two points necessarily include (i, π(i)) or (k, π(k))? Let's examine. The rectangle's row interval is from min(i, k+1) to max(i, k+1). The column interval is from min(π(i)+1, π(k)) to max(π(i)+1, π(k)). If i ≤ k+1, row interval includes rows i,...,k+1. If i ≤ k, then row interval includes row k, which is the row of the uncovered square (k, π(k)). But does the rectangle include column π(k)? The column interval includes π(k) if π(k) is between π(i)+1 and π(k) inclusive. Since π(k) is one of the endpoints, it's included. So if i ≤ k, then the rectangle includes row k and column π(k), thus includes (k, π(k)), which is uncovered. So not allowed. So we need i > k to avoid including row k. But if i > k, then row interval includes rows k+1,...,i. It includes row k+1 (the B point's row) and row i (the A point's row). Does it include row k? No, because k < i and row interval starts at k+1, so row k is excluded. So uncovered square (k, π(k)) is avoided. However, we also need to avoid uncovered square (i, π(i)). Does the rectangle include column π(i)? The column interval includes π(i) if π(i) is between π(i)+1 and π(k). Since π(i) < π(i)+1, it's not in the interval unless π(k) < π(i). Actually the interval is from min(π(i)+1, π(k)) to max(π(i)+1, π(k)). If π(k) < π(i)+1, then the interval is [π(k), π(i)+1] and includes π(i) if π(k) ≤ π(i) ≤ π(i)+1. Since π(i) < π(i)+1, and π(k) ≤ π(i) maybe, then π(i) is included. So we need π(k) > π(i)+1 to avoid including column π(i). So we need π(k) > π(i)+1. So the condition to avoid both uncovered squares is: i > k and π(k) > π(i)+1. This is equivalent to i > k and π(k) > π(i)+1.\n\nNow, note that i > k and π(k) > π(i)+1 implies that 