(j)+1 and columns π(i)+1 to j will not contain any uncovered squares because the uncovered squares are above or left of the rectangle.\n\nBut does such a rectangle exist? Yes, if there are two uncovered squares such that i > π^{-1}(j) and j > π(i). This is possible for many permutations. For example, consider π = identity (i,i). Then π(i) = i, π^{-1}(j) = j. The condition i > π^{-1}(j) becomes i > j, and j > π(i) becomes j > i. These cannot both hold simultaneously. So for identity permutation, no rectangle can contain both a right neighbor and a below neighbor without containing an uncovered square. So the lower bound is tight for identity.\n\nBut for other permutations, maybe we can have a rectangle that contains both a point from A and a point from B. For example, consider π = reverse permutation: π(i) = n+1-i. Then π(i) = n+1-i, π^{-1}(j) = n+1-j. The condition i > π^{-1}(j) becomes i > n+1-j, i + j > n+1. And j > π(i) becomes j > n+1-i, i + j > n+1. So both conditions reduce to i + j > n+1. So any pair (i,j) with i + j > n+1 would satisfy both. So there are many such pairs. So for reverse permutation, there exist rectangles that contain both a point from A and a point from B. Let's test with n=5 for simplicity: π = [5,4,3,2,1]. Then A includes points (i,π(i)+1) for π(i) < n. For i=1, π(1)=5, which is n, so no A point. For i=2, π(2)=4, so A includes (2,5). For i=3, π(3)=3, A includes (3,4). For i=4, π(4)=2, A includes (4,3). For i=5, π(5)=1, A includes (5,2). B includes points (π^{-1}(j)+1, j) for π^{-1}(j) < n. Since π^{-1}(j) = n+1-j, for j=1, π^{-1}(1)=5, no B point. For j=2, π^{-1}(2)=4, B includes (5,2). For j=3, π^{-1}(3)=3, B includes (4,3). For j=4, π^{-1}(4)=2, B includes (3,4). For j=5, π^{-1}(5)=1, B includes (2,5). So A = {(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2)} and B = {(5,2),(4,3),(3,4),(2,5)}. So A and B are the same set, each point appears in both sets. In this case, each point is both a right neighbor of one uncovered square and a below neighbor of another unc