ide \\(L\\).  \nSince the values \\(p(i)-1\\) run through \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), there are\nexactly \\(n-1\\) such cells (all rows except the one with \\(p(i)=1\\)).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nAny axis‑parallel rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) (with \\(I\\) a set of consecutive\nrows and \\(J\\) a set of consecutive columns) that is contained in \\(L\\) can\ncontain at most one rightmost cell \\(r_i\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(c=\\max J\\) be the rightmost column of the rectangle.\nBecause \\(R\\subseteq L\\), every row \\(i\\in I\\) satisfies \\(p(i)>c\\); otherwise\n\\((i,c)\\notin L\\).  Hence for each \\(i\\in I\\) we have \\(p(i)-1\\ge c\\).\nIf a row \\(i\\) satisfies \\(p(i)-1=c\\), then its rightmost cell \\(r_i\\) lies in\ncolumn \\(c\\) and belongs to the rectangle.  Since the numbers \\(p(i)-1\\) are\nall distinct, at most one row of \\(I\\) can satisfy \\(p(i)-1=c\\).  Therefore\n\\(R\\) contains at most one rightmost cell. ∎\n\n---\n\nBecause the \\(n-1\\) rightmost cells are all distinct, Lemma 1 implies that any\nfamily of rectangles covering \\(L\\) must contain at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(L)\\ge n-1},\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(f(L)\\) denotes the minimum number of rectangles needed to tile \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the right part \\(R\\)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<n\\) define the **leftmost** cell of \\(R\\) by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=(i,p(i)+1).\n\\]\n\nAgain there are exactly \\(n-1\\) such cells (all rows except the one with\n\\(p(i)=n\\)).\n\nThe same argument as in Lemma 1, applied to the leftmost column of a rectangle,\ngives:\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nAny rectangle contained in \\(R\\) contains at most one leftmost cell \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nConsequently any tiling of \\(R\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(R)\\ge n-1}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Global lower bound\n\nSince the two parts are disjoint, any tiling of the whole set \\(C\\) uses at\nleast  \n\n\\[\nf(L)+f(R)\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFix the permutation \\(p\\) from the statement.  \n\n*Left part.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{L}= \\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)>j\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause exactly the rows with \\(p(i)>j\\) have a cell in column \\(j\\) of \\(L\\),\n\\(T_j^{L}\\) is a vertical rectangle of width 1 and height \\(n-j\\).  The\ndifferent \\(T_j^{L}\\) occupy distinct columns, hence are pairwise disjoint, and\ntheir union is precisely \\(L\\).\n\n*Right part.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{R}= \\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)<j\\}.\n\\]\n\nHere the rows with \\(p(i)<j\\) are exactly those that have a cell in column\n\\(j\\) of \\(R\\); thus \\(T_j^{R}\\) is a vertical rectangle of width 1 and height\n\\(j-1\\).  Again the rectangles for different \\(j\\) are disjoint and together\ncover \\(R\\).\n\nThe two families \\(\\{T_j^{L}\\}\\) and \\(\\{T_j^{R}\\}\\) are disjoint because they\nuse different columns.  Altogether we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{T_j^{L}\\}\\,|=n-1,\\qquad |\\,\\{T_j^{R}\\}\\,|=n-1,\n\\]\n\nso the construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles and satisfies all requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (3) and the explicit construction coincide; therefore the\nminimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nReview the “Summary’’ and “Detailed Solution’’ sections to ensure:\n\n* All mathematical statements are enclosed in TeX delimiters.\n* The logical flow is complete: permutation 