                "content": "**Solution Explanation**\n\nFor an integer \\(n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nB_n=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of unit squares of an \\(n\\times n\\) board.\nA *tile* is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; it is identified with the\nset of squares it covers.\nA placement of tiles is a **partition** of a set of squares – no two tiles may\noverlap and every square that is covered belongs to exactly one tile.\n\nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.\nHence the uncovered squares form a *permutation matrix*:\nthere is a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered\nsquares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares, i.e. \\(B_n\\setminus U\\), have to be tiled.\nFor a set \\(X\\subseteq B_n\\) denote by \\(\\operatorname{rect}(X)\\) the minimum\nnumber of rectangles needed to partition \\(X\\).\n\nThe problem asks for  \n\n\\[\n\\min_{\\sigma\\in S_n}\\operatorname{rect}\\bigl(B_n\\setminus U_\\sigma\\bigr)\n\\qquad\\text{with }n=2025 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 1.  Upper bound – a construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nTake the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nThe uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\).\n\n*Above the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\;(2\\le j\\le n)\\) the squares \\((1,j),(2,j),\\dots ,(j-1,j)\\)\nform a vertical strip.  These \\(n-1\\) strips are pairwise disjoint and together\ncover every square with column index larger than its row index.\n\n*Below the diagonal.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(2\\le i\\le n)\\) the squares \\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1)\\)\nform a horizontal strip.  These \\(n-1\\) strips are disjoint and together\ncover every square with row index larger than its column index.\n\nThus the whole board except the diagonal squares is tiled with  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rect}\\bigl(B_n\\setminus U_{\\text{diag}}\\bigr)\\le 2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  Geometry of the uncovered squares  \n\nFor any permutation \\(\\sigma\\) each uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) is\nisolated – it does not share a side with another uncovered square.\nConsequently the boundary of the set \\(B_n\\setminus U_\\sigma\\) consists of\n\n* the outer boundary of the whole board (a convex rectangle), and  \n* for every uncovered square four **inner corners** (the four 270° corners\n  of the little “hole’’ left by that square).\n\nIf a hole lies on the outer border of the board one of its four corners\ncoincides with a convex corner of the board; such a corner is not an inner\ncorner.  A hole on a side (but not a corner) contributes exactly **two**\ninner corners; a hole strictly inside the board contributes **four** inner\ncorners.\n\nBecause each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square,\nat most one hole can be placed on each side of the board.\nHence at most two holes can be corner‑holes.\nAll remaining \\(n-4\\) holes are interior holes.\nTherefore the total number \\(C\\) of inner corners satisfies\n\n\\[\nC\\ge 4\\,(n-4)+2\\cdot 2 = 4n-8 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  How many inner corners can one tile contain?  \n\nConsider a rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) (with \\(I,J\\) non‑empty sets of rows\nand columns).  \nSince no uncovered square may belong to a tile, for every row \\(i\\in I\\)\nits uncovered column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) is **outside** \\(J\\); similarly for every\ncolumn \\(j\\in J\\) the uncovered row \\(\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\) lies outside \\(I\\).\nHence either\n\n* all uncovered columns of rows in \\(I\\) lie to the **right** of \\(J\\), or  \n* they all lie to the **left** of \\(J\\).\n\nAnalogously, all uncovered rows of columns in \\(J\\) lie either **above** or\n**below** \\(I\\).\n\nConsequently a rectangle can be situated only in one of the four\nquadrants determined by the uncovered squares:\n\n```\n   above          below\n left   R   right   left   R   right\n```\n\nIn each case the rectangle can meet the boundary of the board only at two\nopposite corners: a **bottom‑left** corner (adjacent to a hole on its right)\nor a **top‑right** corner (adjacent to a hole above it).  \nA rectangle can never contain a hole, therefore it can contain **at most one**\ninner corner of each type, i.e. **at most two** inner corners in total.\nMoreover, if a rectangle contains two inner corners they must be opposite\ncorners of the rectangle; this forces the rectangle to be a *strip*\n(one row high or one column wide).\n\nThus for any tiling\n\n\\[\n\\text{(number of tiles)}\\;\\ge\\; \\frac{C}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Lower bound – at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nInsert the estimate (2) for \\(C\\) into (3):\n\n\\[\n\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\; \\frac{4n-8}{2}=2n-4 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIt remains to show that the extra two tiles are unavoidable.\nAssume a tiling uses exactly \\(2n-4\\) rectangles.\nThen equality must hold in (3), so every rectangle must contain **exactly**\ntwo inner corners, and therefore every rectangle is a strip.\nA strip can cover at most one inner corner on each of the two sides of the\nboard.  Because there are only two side‑holes (the four border holes give\nexactly two inner corners on the sides), at most two strips can be placed\nalong the sides.  The remaining \\(2n-6\\) strips would have to lie completely\ninside the board, but an interior strip can contain only **one** inner corner\n(the one belonging to the hole that lies immediately to its right or above it).\nHence the interior strips would cover at most \\(2n-6\\) inner corners, while\nthe interior holes contribute \\(4(n-4)\\) inner corners – a contradiction for\n\\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nTherefore a tiling needs **strictly more** than \\(2n-4\\) rectangles; together\nwith the trivial bound \\(\\text{tiles}\\ge n\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2n-2 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  Conclusion for \\(n=2025\\)\n\nFrom (1) we have a construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and from (5) we have a\nmatching lower bound.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Answer:**  The smallest number of rectangular tiles that guarantees one\nuncovered square in each row and each column of a \\(2025\\times2025\\) board\nis  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
