al \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile covering the squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>i\\).  \n   - For each column \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a vertical \\((n-j)\\times 1\\) tile covering the squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\).\n\n   The two families of tiles are disjoint, they cover every square except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), and their total number is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number; hence it is optimal.  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the rows and columns of the board by \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\in S_n\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The two regions \\(L\\) and \\(R\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j<\\pi(i)\\},\n\\qquad \nR:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery covered square belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\); the two sets are disjoint.  \n\n*Claim.*  Any axis‑parallel rectangle that contains no uncovered square is entirely contained in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let a rectangle be determined by a row interval \\(I=[a,b]\\) and a column interval \\(J=[c,d]\\) (both inclusive).  \nIf the rectangle contained a square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<\\pi(i)\\) (i.e. belonging to \\(L\\)) and also a square \\((i',j')\\) with \\(j'>\\pi(i')\\) (i.e. belonging to \\(R\\)), then for the same row \\(i\\) we would have \\(c\\le j<\\pi(i)<j'\\le d\\), which forces \\(\\pi(i)\\in J\\); consequently the square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) would lie inside the rectangle – a contradiction.  Hence the rectangle cannot intersect both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\). ∎\n\nThus a tiling of the board (with the uncovered squares left empty) induces a partition of \\(L\\) into rectangles and a partition of \\(R\\) into rectangles.  The total number of tiles equals  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{rectangles covering }L\\;+\\;\\#\\text{rectangles covering }R .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for a Ferrers diagram\n\nWe treat \\(L\\); the same argument applies to \\(R\\).\n\nOrder the rows by increasing values of \\(\\pi(i)\\).  \nLet this order be \\(i_1,i_2,\\dots ,i_n\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(i_1)<\\pi(i_2)<\\dots <\\pi(i_n).\n\\]\n\nFor a row \\(i_k\\) the set of columns belonging to \\(L\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\pi(i_k)-1\\},\n\\]\n\nhence its *length* (the number of squares of \\(L\\) in that row) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_k:=\\pi(i_k)-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause the \\(\\pi(i_k)\\) are a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nConsequently, after the ordering we obtain a Ferrers diagram whose row lengths are  \n\n\\[\n0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA Ferrers diagram with strictly increasing row lengths has the following property.\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let a Ferrers diagram have row lengths \\(\\lambda_1<\\lambda_2<\\dots <\\lambda_m\\) (all positive).  \n> In any partition of this diagram into axis‑parallel rectangles, at least \\(m\\) rectangles are required.\n\n*Proof of Lemma.*  \nConsider the rightmost square of each row, i.e. the square \\((r,\\lambda_r)\\) in row \\(r\\).  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(R\\) contains the rightmost squares of two distinct rows, say rows \\(p<q\\).  \nSince the rectangle’s column interval must be contiguous, its rightmost column is at most \\(\\lambda_p\\) (the smaller of the two row lengths).  \nHence column \\(\\lambda_q\\) (the rightmost column of row \\(q\\)) cannot belong to \\(R\\) – a contradiction.  \nThus each rectangle can 