ain the rightmost square of **at most one** row.  \nSince there are \\(m\\) rows, at least \\(m\\) rectangles are needed. ∎\n\nApplying the lemma to the diagram (1) we have \\(m=n-1\\) non‑empty rows (the row of length \\(0\\) is empty).  \nTherefore any partition of \\(L\\) into rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nExactly the same reasoning applied to \\(R\\) (order the rows by decreasing \\(\\pi(i)\\); the row lengths become \\(n-\\pi(i)\\), i.e. again \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\)) yields the lower bound  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{rectangles covering }R\\;\\ge\\;n-1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently every admissible tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nTake the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{h}}:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle.  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{v}}:=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\((n-j)\\times 1\\) rectangle.\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n* The tiles are pairwise disjoint: a horizontal tile lies entirely in the set \\(\\{(i,j):i<j\\}\\) while a vertical tile lies in \\(\\{(i,j):i>j\\}\\); the two sets are disjoint.  \n* Every square with \\(i\\neq j\\) belongs either to a horizontal tile (if \\(i<j\\)) or to a vertical tile (if \\(i>j\\)).  \n* The squares \\((i,i)\\) (the diagonal) are not covered, and there is exactly one such square in each row and each column.\n\nThe number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{horizontal}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{vertical}}=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the bound (2) is achievable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final computation\n\nFor the given board \\(n=