re the minimum number of tiles is \\(n=2025\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  A placement of rectangular tiles satisfying the statement can be described as follows.\n\n#### 1. Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nSince each of the \\(n\\) rows must contain exactly one uncovered unit square and each of the \\(n\\) columns must also contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a set of \\(n\\) cells with precisely one in each row and one in each column.  Hence there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the binary matrix \\(A=(a_{ij})\\) by  \n\n\\[\na_{ij}=\n\\begin{cases}\n0, & (i,j)\\in U,\\\\[2mm]\n1, & \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(J\\) denote the \\(n\\times n\\) all\u2011ones matrix and let \\(P\\) be the permutation matrix of \\(p\\) (i.e. \\(P_{ij}=1\\) iff \\(j=p(i)\\)).  Then\n\n\\[\nA = J-P. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the entries equal \\(1\\) exactly on the cells covered by tiles.\n\n#### 2. Construction with \\(n\\) tiles (upper bound)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place a single horizontal tile covering all squares of that row except the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\).  Formally, the \\(i\\)-th tile covers  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq p(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach tile is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-1)\\); the tiles lie in distinct rows, so they are pairwise disjoint.  Their union covers precisely the cells where \\(a_{ij}=1\\).  Hence a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles exists.\n\n#### 3. Lower bound via matrix rank\n\n**(a) Tiles as rank\u20111 matrices.**  \nA rectangular tile covering a set of rows \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and a set of columns \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) corresponds to the binary matrix  \n\n\\[\nB_{IJ}= \\mathbf{1}_I\\mathbf{1}_J^{\\!\\top},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{1}_I\\) (resp. \\(\\mathbf{1}_J\\)) is the column (resp. row) indicator vector of the set \\(I\\) (resp. \\(J\\)).  This matrix has rank \\(1\\) over \\(\\mathbb{R}\\).\n\n**(b) Decomposition of \\(A\\).**  \nIf the tiling uses \\(t\\) tiles with row/column sets \\((I_k,J_k)\\) for \\(k=1,\\dots ,t\\), the disjointness of the tiles implies  \n\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{k=1}^{t} B_{I_kJ_k}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, each entry of the right\u2011hand side is the number of tiles covering that cell; because each cell is covered at most once, the entry is either \\(0\\) (uncovered) or \\(1\\) (covered), matching \\(A\\).\n\n**(c) Rank inequality.**  \nFor any matrices \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_t\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}\\!\\Bigl(\\sum_{k=1}^{t}M_k\\Bigr)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}(M_k).\n\\]\n\nApplying this to (2) and using \\(\\operatorname{rank}(B_{I_kJ_k})=1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le t. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus the number of tiles is at least the rank of \\(A\\).\n\n**(d) Computing \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\).**  \nFrom (1), \\(A=J-P\\).  Suppose \\((J-P)x=0\\) for some vector \\(x\\in\\mathbb{R}^n\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\nJx = Px. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe left side \\(Jx\\) equals \\((\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i)\\mathbf{1}\\), where \\(\\mathbf{1}\\) is the all\u2011ones vector.  The right side \\(Px\\) is a permutation of the coordinates of \\(x\\).  Hence every coordinate of \\(Px\\) (and therefore of \\(x\\)) equals the same constant  \n\n\\[\nc:=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(x=c\\mathbf{1}\\).  Substituting into (4) yields  \n\n\\[\nn c\\,\\mathbf{1}=c\\,\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (n-1)c=0.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n>1\\), we obtain \\(c=0\\) and consequently \\(x=0\\).  Therefore the nullspace of \\(J-P\\) is trivial, so \\(J-P\\) is invertible and  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)=\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)=n. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n**(e) Consequence.**  \nCombining (3) and (5) gives  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus any tiling satisfying the problem\u2019s conditions must use at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n#### 4. Optimality\n\nThe construction of \u00a72 uses exactly \\(n\\) tiles, and inequality (6) shows that fewer than \\(n\\) tiles are impossible.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\n- Verify that the uncovered squares indeed form a permutation matrix; the argument uses the \u201cexactly one uncovered per row and column\u201d condition.\n- Confirm that each rectangular tile corresponds to a rank\u20111 binary matrix via the outer product of its row\u2011indicator and column\u2011indicator vectors.\n- Ensure the disjointness condition guarantees that the sum of these rank\u20111 matrices equals the covering matrix \\(A\\) with entries \\(0\\) (uncovered) and \\(1\\) (covered).\n- Re\u2011check the rank computation for \\(A=J-P\\): the only solution of \\((J-P)x=0\\) is the zero vector, establishing full rank \\(n\\).\n- Confirm that the construction uses one horizontal tile per row (size \\(1\\times (n-1)\\)), covering all cells except the designated uncovered square, thereby giving exactly one uncovered square per column as well.\n- Confirm that the final answer \\(\\boxed{2025}\\) follows directly from matching upper and lower bounds."
