ratorname{rank}(A)+\\operatorname{rank}(B)\\).  \nApplying this repeatedly to (3) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(C)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}(M_{T_k})\n                 =\\sum_{k=1}^{t}1 = t .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(t\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(C)\\).  We now compute \\(\\operatorname{rank}(C)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1.  The matrix \\(J-P\\) is nonsingular; hence \\(\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)=n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\((J-P)x=0\\) for some column vector \\(x\\in\\mathbb R^{n}\\).  \nLet \\(s=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i\\).  Then \\(Jx = s\\,(1,1,\\dots ,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\).  \nThus the equation \\((J-P)x=0\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\ns\\,(1,1,\\dots ,1)^{\\mathsf T}=Px .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) merely permutes the coordinates of \\(x\\), (5) says that each coordinate of \\(Px\\) equals the same number \\(s\\).  Hence every entry of \\(x\\) equals \\(s\\).  Substituting \\(x_i=s\\) into the definition of \\(s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ns = \\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i = ns .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n>1\\), we obtain \\(s=0\\) and consequently \\(x=0\\).  The only solution of \\((J-P)x=0\\) is the zero vector, so \\(J-P\\) is invertible and has full rank \\(n\\). ∎\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we have \\(\\operatorname{rank}(C)=\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)=n\\).  Combining with (4) gives the lower bound  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge n = 2025 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nSelect any permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (for instance the identity).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) place a rectangular tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\bigl(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\sigma(i)\\}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n\\(T_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle; it covers every square of row \\(i\\) except the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nThe tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_n\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct 