identity permutation):**  \n   Take \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \n   - For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal rectangle covering the squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\) (size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\)).  \n   - For each \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a vertical rectangle covering the squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\) (size \\((n-j)\\times 1\\)).  \n\n   These \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, cover every square except the diagonal ones, and respect the grid\u2011line condition.  \n\n6. **Conclusion:**  \n   The lower bound \\(2(n-1)\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number, so the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   2(2025-1)=2\\cdot 2024=4048.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA unit square of the \\(n\\times n\\) grid will be denoted by its row\u2013column coordinates \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nSince each row must contain exactly one uncovered square, there is a function  \n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n\\text{such that the uncovered square in row }i\\text{ is }(i,\\pi(i)).\n\\]\nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\pi\\) is a bijection; i.e. a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Two disjoint regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad \nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery covered square belongs either to \\(R\\) or to \\(L\\); the two sets are disjoint because a square cannot simultaneously satisfy \\(j>\\pi(i)\\) and \\(j<\\pi(i)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Ferrers\u2011diagram structure  \n\nOrder the rows by increasing values of \\(\\pi(i)\\).  \nLet \\(\\sigma\\) be the permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(\\sigma(1))<\\pi(\\sigma(2))<\\dots <\\pi(\\sigma(n)).\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(k\\) the set of squares of \\(R\\) in row \\(\\sigma(k)\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\{(\\sigma(k),j)\\mid j=\\pi(\\sigma(k))+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has length  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_k=n-\\pi(\\sigma(k)).\n\\]\n\nBecause the \\(\\pi(\\sigma(k))\\) are strictly increasing, the lengths \\(\\ell_k\\) are **strictly decreasing**:\n\\[\n\\ell_1>\\ell_2>\\dots >\\ell_n .\n\\]\nExactly one of the \\(\\ell_k\\) equals \\(0\\) (the row for which \\(\\pi(i)=n\\)). Hence \\(R\\) is a right\u2011justified Ferrers diagram with \\(n-1\\) non\u2011empty rows whose lengths are distinct.\n\nPerform the same ordering in decreasing order of \\(\\pi(i)\\); then \\(L\\) becomes a left\u2011justified Ferrers diagram with \\(n-1\\) non\u2011empty rows, again with distinct lengths.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma (rectangles in a Ferrers diagram)\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let \\(F\\) be a Ferrers diagram (a set of unit squares) whose non\u2011empty rows have pairwise distinct lengths. Any partition of \\(F\\) into axis\u2011aligned rectangles uses at least as many rectangles as there are non\u2011empty rows of \\(F\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(F\\) is left\u2011justified; the right\u2011justified case is symmetric (use the leftmost cells).  \nLet the non\u2011empty rows be numbered \\(1,\\dots ,m\\) and let the length of row \\(r\\) be \\(L_r\\) with \\(L_1>L_2>\\dots >L_m\\).  \nConsider the right\u2011most cell of each row, namely \\((r,L_r)\\). Because the lengths are distinct, the columns \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) are all different.  \n\nA rectangle is a product of a set of consecutive rows and a set of consecutive columns. Consequently a rectangle can contain at most one of the cells \\((r,L_r)\\); otherwise it would contain two cells in the same column, contradicting 