ares: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(4,4). Rectangle rows 1-4, columns 2-3 includes (2,2) and (3,3) uncovered? Actually (2,2) column 2 row 2 is uncovered, (3,3) column 3 row 3 is uncovered. So not allowed.\n\nThus crossing seems impossible for identity permutation because any rectangle covering rows i..j with column interval [c_left, c_right] that includes both columns > i and columns < j will inevitably include some uncovered square (some k between i and j where pi(k)=k is within the column interval). Indeed, if column interval contains both a column > i and a column < j, then the interval contains some integer k such that i < k < j (by integer intermediate value). Then the uncovered square (k,k) lies in the rectangle (since row k is covered, column k is within interval). So rectangle would contain uncovered square, not allowed. So crossing is impossible for identity permutation. But for a different permutation, maybe crossing is possible? Let's examine a permutation that is not monotonic, e.g., pi = (2,1,4,3) for n=4. Then uncovered squares: (1,2),(2,1),(3,4),(4,3). The shape is like a \"cross\". Could we have a rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 1-2? That rectangle includes (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2). Uncovered squares are (1,2) and (2,1). So rectangle includes uncovered squares, not allowed. How about rectangle rows 1-2, columns 3-4? That includes squares (1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4). Uncovered squares are not there (since uncovered squares are in columns 1,2,3,4 but only at positions (1,2),(2,1),(3,4),(4,3)). So rectangle rows 1-2, columns 3-4 is allowed and lies in R for rows 1-2? Actually for row 1, pi(1)=2, columns 3-4 >2, so right side; row 2, pi(2)=1, columns 3-4 >1, also right side. So rectangle is wholly right side for both rows.\n\nNow consider rectangle rows 1-3, columns 2-3. Row 1: pi(1)=2, columns 2-3 includes column 2 which is uncovered (1,2). So not allowed.\n\nWhat about rectangle rows 1-3, columns 1-2? Row 1: includes column 2 uncovered; not allowed.\n\nThus cross