n\n\\(b<c\\) that contains \\((i,i+1)\\).  Write its row interval as \\([a,b]\\) and its\ncolumn interval as \\([c,d]\\).  Because \\((i,i+1)\\in R\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na\\le i\\le b,\\qquad c\\le i+1\\le d.\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(b<c\\) and \\(c\\le i+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le i\\).  Together with\n\\(i\\le b\\) this forces \\(b=i\\).  Consequently also \\(a=i\\); otherwise the\nrow interval would contain a row \\(<i\\) and then \\(b=i\\) would not be the\nlargest row.  Hence the row interval of \\(R\\) is the single row \\(\\{i\\}\\).  In\nother words, **any rectangle covering the cell \\((i,i+1)\\) is a horizontal\n\\(1\\times w\\) tile lying entirely in row \\(i\\).**\n\nSince the rows \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) are distinct, the rectangles covering the\ncells \\((i,i+1)\\) are pairwise distinct.  Therefore at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles\nare required to cover the whole upper triangular region.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound for the lower triangular region  \n\nThe lower triangular region  \n\n\\[\nL_T=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nis the transpose of \\(U_T\\).  By symmetry, the topmost cell of column\n\\(j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\)) is \\((j+1,j)\\).  An argument identical to the one\nin the previous paragraph shows that any rectangle lying below the diagonal\nand containing \\((j+1,j)\\) must be confined to the single column \\(\\{j\\}\\).\nHence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for \\(L_T\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Combined lower bound  \n\nEvery tiling of the board that leaves exactly one uncovered square in each\nrow and column must cover \\(U_T\\) and \\(L_T\\) separately.  Consequently the\ntotal number \\(t\\) of rectangles satisfies  \n\n\\[\nt\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction achieving the bound  \n\nKeep the uncovered squares on the diagonal (2).  Define the following\nrectangles:\n\n*For the upper region:* for each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{\\,\\text{row }i\\,\\}\\times\\{\\,\\text{columns }i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\,\\},\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle.\n\n*For the lower region:* for each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{\\,\\text{rows }j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}\\times\\{\\,\\text{column }j\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nan \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle.\n\nEach cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) belongs to \\(H_i\\); each cell with\n\\(i>j\\) belongs to \\(V_j\\).  The rectangles are pairwise disjoint, none of\nthem contains a diagonal cell, and together they cover every non\u2011diagonal\nsquare.  The total number of rectangles used is  \n\n\\[\n| \\{H_i\\}|+|\\{V_j\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the bound (4) is tight.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer  \n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
