square in column \\(j\\) is \\((q(j),j)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Two disjoint regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j<p(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR=\\{(i,j):1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j>p(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set of covered squares is exactly \\(L\\cup R\\) and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nNo rectangle (tile) can contain a cell of \\(L\\) and a cell of \\(R\\) simultaneously.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(T\\) contains \\((i,j)\\in L\\) and \\((i',j')\\in R\\).  \nLet the column interval of \\(T\\) be \\([c,d]\\) with \\(c\\le j\\le d\\) and \\(c\\le j'\\le d\\).  \nSince \\((i,j)\\in L\\) we have \\(j<p(i)\\); since \\((i',j')\\in R\\) we have \\(j'>p(i')\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nc\\le j < p(i)\\le j' \\le d .\n\\]\n\nThus the column \\(p(i)\\) lies in \\([c,d]\\).  Because row \\(i\\) belongs to the row interval of \\(T\\), the cell \\((i,p(i))\\) would be inside \\(T\\), contradicting that this cell is uncovered.  \u220e\n\n\n\nConsequently the family of tiles splits into  \n\n* **left\u2011tiles** (subsets of \\(L\\)), and  \n* **right\u2011tiles** (subsets of \\(R\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Ordering the rows  \n\nRelabel the rows so that  \n\n\\[\np(1)<p(2)<\\dots <p(n). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n(Only the relative order matters; relabelling does not affect the number of tiles.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nLet \\(T\\) be a left\u2011tile.  Then there exist integers  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad d\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{k,k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{1,2,\\dots ,d\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the row set of \\(T\\) as a consecutive interval \\(I=\\{a,a+1,\\dots ,b\\}\\) and its column interval as \\([c,d]\\) with \\(c\\le d\\).  \nSince \\(T\\subseteq L\\), for every \\(i\\in I\\) we have \\([c,d]\\subseteq[1,p(i)-1]\\); in particular \\(d<p(i)\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nd<\\min_{i\\in I}p(i). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause of the ordering (1), the inequality (3) forces \\(I\\) to be a suffix of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\): there exists \\(k\\) such that \\(I=\\{k,k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nMoreover, (3) implies \\(c\\) can be taken equal to \\(1\\); otherwise we could shrink the tile horizontally without losing any covered square.  Hence \\(c=1\\) and (2) holds. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=(i,p(i)-1) \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nbelongs to \\(L\\) and is the **rightmost** cell of row \\(i\\) in \\(L\\).  We call \\(\\ell_i\\) a *left corner*.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy definition of \\(L\\) we have \\(p(i)-1<p(i)\\), so \\(\\ell_i\\in L\\).  No cell of row \\(i\\) with a larger column can belong to \\(L\\), because any column \\(\\ge p(i)\\) is not strictly smaller than \\(p(i)\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4  \nEvery left\u2011tile contains exactly one left corner, namely the left corner belonging to its topmost row.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(T\\) be a left\u2011tile described by (2).  Its topmost row is \\(k\\).  The rightmost column of \\(T\\) is \\(d\\); therefore the cell \\((k,d)\\) lies in \\(T\\).  Because of (2) we have \\(d<p(k)\\); consequently \\(d\\le p(k)-1\\).  By Lemma\u202f3 the cell \\((k,p(k)-1)\\) is the unique rightmost cell of row \\(k\\) in \\(L\\).  Since \\(d\\) is the maximal column of \\(T\\), we must have \\(d=p(k)-1\\), and thus \\((k,d)=\\ell_k\\).  No other row of \\(T\\) can contribute a left corner, because for any row \\(i>k\\) the rightmost column of \\(T\\) is still \\(d\\), which is strictly left of the hole \\(p(i)\\); hence the rightmost cell of row \\(i\\) in \\(L\\) lies at column \\(p(i)-1> d\\) and is not in \\(T\\).  Hence \\(T\\) contains precisely the left corner \\(\\ell_k\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary 5  \nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) left corners (all rows except the unique row with \\(p(i)=1\\)), and consequently at least \\(n-1\\) left\u2011tiles are required to cover \\(L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(p\\) is a permutation, exactly one index \\(i\\) satisfies \\(p(i)=1\\); for that row the set \\(\\{j:j<p(i)\\}\\) is empty, so no left corner arises.  For every other row \\(i\\) we obtain a distinct left corner \\(\\ell_i\\) because the second coordinate \\(p(i)-1\\) is distinct (the numbers \\(p(i)-1\\) run through \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)).  By Lemma\u202f4 each left\u2011tile contains a unique left corner, so covering all left corners forces at least \\(n-1\\) left\u2011tiles. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The right region  \n\nReflect the board horizontally (replace column \\(j\\) by column \\(n+1-j\\)).  Under this reflection the right region \\(R\\) becomes the left region of the reflected board, and the same reasoning as above shows that at least \\(n-1\\) right\u2011tiles are needed to cover \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 6 (Lower bound)  \nAny tiling satisfying the problem\u2019s condition uses at least  \n\n\\[\n( n-1)+( n-1)=2n-2\n\\]  \n\ntiles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 the tiles split into left\u2011tiles covering \\(L\\) and right\u2011tiles covering \\(R\\).  Corollary\u202f5 gives a lower bound of \\(n-1\\) for the left\u2011tiles; the symmetric argument gives the same bound for the right\u2011tiles.  Adding the two bounds yields the claim. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation  \n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad (1\\le i\\le n),\n\\]\n\ni.e. the uncovered squares are the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left side.*  \nFor each row \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{left}}=\\{i\\}\\times\\{1,2,\\dots ,i-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) tile covering the whole left part of that row.\n\n*Right side.*  \nFor each row \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{right}}=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) tile covering the whole right part of that row.\n\nThe two families are disjoint (they occupy different rows or, when they share a row, they occupy disjoint column intervals).  Altogether they consist of  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]  \n\ntiles and cover every cell of the board except the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  Hence each row and each column has exactly one uncovered square, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 7 (Upper bound)  \nThe board can be tiled with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe construction described above provides an explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles, satisfying all the conditions. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nLemmas\u202f6 and\u202f7 together show that the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  For the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\min\\text{ tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nThus the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
