eft} = {i} x {1,...,i-1} and T_i^{right} = {i} x {i+1,...,n}. These are explicit specifications (concrete details). So they are Category C, valid method. So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality. We need to see if there are any justification gaps or critical errors. The solution seems fairly rigorous. However we need to check if any steps are missing justification or have gaps. Let's examine each part.\n\n- The solution defines left neighbours L_i and right neighbours R_i. It claims that #L_i = n-1, #R_i = n-1, and all cells L_i (resp. R_i) are distinct. That's correct: because each row i has a left neighbour unless p(i)=1; exactly one row has p(i)=1, so n-1 left neighbours. Similarly for right neighbours. Distinctness: Since p is a permutation, each L_i = (i,p(i)-1) has unique column p(i)-1; distinct columns because p(i) are distinct, so L_i are distinct. So fine.\n\n- Lemma 1: If a tile contains a left neighbour L_i, then the tile is left of every hole in the rows it covers. The proof: L_i lies in column p(i)-1; tile contains column p(i)-1 but must not contain hole (i,p(i)), so its rightmost column d < p(i). If tile covered a row k with p(k) <= d, then hole (k,p(k)) would be covered, contradiction. So d < p(k) for all rows k in I. This is valid reasoning.\n\n- Lemma 2: A left-tile contains at most one left neighbour. The proof: Let left-tile cover rows I and columns [c,d]. Since d < p(k) for all k in I, let i0 be the row in I with minimal p(k). Then for any other row k != i0, p(k) > p(i0) > d, so p(k)-1 >= d+1 > d, so left neighbour (k,p(k)-1) lies in column > d, not covered. The only possible left neighbour inside tile is that of row i0, which belongs to tile exactly when d = p(i0)-1. So at most one left neighbour. This is valid.\n\n- The argument that a tile cannot be both left-tile and right-tile is correct: left-tile requires d < p(k) for all rows covered; right-tile requires c > p(k) for all rows covered; cannot have both unless no rows covered