ailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA unit square is identified by its row and column indices \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nEach row contains exactly one uncovered square; denote its column by \\(p(i)\\).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a bijection, i.e. a permutation.  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Every tile lies completely either left or right of all holes in its rows  \n\nConsider a tile \\(T=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) (with \\(a\\le b,\\;c\\le d\\)).  \nIf \\(T\\) contained a hole \\((i,p(i))\\) with \\(a\\le i\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le p(i)\\le d\\), the tile would be illegal.  \nHence for every row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) we must have either  \n\n\\[\nd<p(i)\\qquad\\text{(the whole tile is left of the hole in row }i\\text{)},\n\\tag{2a}\n\\]\nor  \n\n\\[\nc>p(i)\\qquad\\text{(the whole tile is right of the hole in row }i\\text{)}.\n\\tag{2b}\n\\]\n\nSuppose there exist rows \\(i_1,i_2\\in[a,b]\\) with \\(d<p(i_1)\\) and \\(c>p(i_2)\\).  \nThen \\(p(i_1)>d\\ge c>p(i_2)\\).  \nSince the values \\(\\{p(i)\\mid a\\le i\\le b\\}\\) are distinct integers, some index \\(i_3\\) between \\(i_1\\) and \\(i_2\\) satisfies \\(c\\le p(i_3)\\le d\\); consequently \\((i_3,p(i_3))\\in T\\), contradicting the definition of a tile.  \nTherefore **all rows of a tile satisfy the same inequality**; every tile is contained either in  \n\n\\[\nL:=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<p(i)\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nor in  \n\n\\[\nR:=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>p(i)\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently a tiling of the whole board splits into a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\); the total number of tiles is the sum of the numbers of tiles used for \\(L\\) and for \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Description of the two regions  \n\nIn region \\(L\\) the \\(i\\)-th row consists of the left\u2011justified block of length  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=p(i)-1\\qquad (0\\le\\ell_i\\le n-1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIn region \\(R\\) the \\(i\\)-th row consists of the right\u2011justified block of length  \n\n\\[\nr_i:=n-p(i)\\qquad (0\\le r_i\\le n-1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are \u201cskyline\u2019\u2019 shapes: each row is a contiguous interval of unit squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Skyline Lemma  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\quad\n\\text{Let }h_1,\\dots ,h_n\\ge0\\text{ and }\nS=\\{(i,j)\\mid1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le h_i\\}.\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{The minimum number of axis\u2011parallel rectangles that partition }S\n\\text{ equals }\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,h_i-h_{i-1}\\},\n\\quad h_0:=0.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* Scan the rows from top to bottom.  \nWhen the height rises from \\(h_{i-1}\\) to \\(h_i\\) we must start \\(h_i-h_{i-1}\\) new vertical rectangles; when the height falls we simply stop some of the already existing rectangles.  \nThe described greedy construction uses exactly the displayed number of rectangles, and no construction can use fewer because each increase forces the creation of a new rectangle. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound for the number of tiles  \n\nApply the lemma to \\(L\\) with heights \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nUsing \\(\\ell_i-\\ell_{i-1}=p(i)-p(i-1)\\) (and setting \\(p(0):=0\\)) we obtain\n\n\\[\nN_L=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\ell_i-\\ell_{i-1}\\}\n    =\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,p(i)-p(i-1)\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nApply the lemma to \\(R\\) with heights \\(r_i\\).  \nSince \\(r_i-r_{i-1}=-(p(i)-p(i-1))\\),\n\n\\[\nN_R=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,r_i-r_{i-1}\\}\n    =\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,p(i-1)-p(i)\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAdding (7) and (8) and using \\(p(0)=0\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_L+N_R\n&= \\bigl(p(1)-1\\bigr)+(n-p(1))\n   +\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl(\\max\\{0,p(i)-p(i-1)\\}\n                       +\\max\\{0,p(i-1)-p(i)\\}\\bigr)\\\\\n&= (n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}|p(i)-p(i-1)|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  A universal inequality for permutations  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma.}\\qquad\n\\sum_{i=2}^{n}|p(i)-p(i-1)|\\ge n-1,\n\\]\nwith equality exactly for the monotone permutations \\(p(i)=i\\) and \\(p(i)=n+1-i\\).\n\n*Proof.* The numbers \\(p(1),p(2),\\dots ,p(n)\\) are a rearrangement of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nStarting from \\(p(1)\\) and moving to \\(p(2),p(3),\\dots ,p(n)\\) we must cross the distance between the smallest and the largest element, which is \\(n-1\\).  \nFormally, each of the \\(n-1\\) gaps \\(\\{k,k+1\\}\\) (\\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)) must be crossed at least once when moving from one term of the sequence to the next, contributing at least \\(1\\) to the sum for each gap. Hence the total is at least \\(n-1\\).  \nIf the sum equals \\(n-1\\), every step must be of size exactly \\(1\\); thus the sequence is strictly monotone, i.e. either increasing (\\(p(i)=i\\)) or decreasing (\\(p(i)=n+1-i\\)). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Final lower bound  \n\nInsert the inequality of Lemma\u202f6 into (9):\n\n\\[\nN_L+N_R\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSince any tiling of the whole board uses at least \\(N_L+N_R\\) tiles, we have proved that **every admissible tiling uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)).  \nThen \\(\\ell_i=i-1\\) and \\(r_i=n-i\\).\n\n*Left region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) the set \\(\\{(i,j):i>j\\}\\) is a vertical rectangle of height \\(n-j\\). These \\(n-1\\) rectangles partition \\(L\\).\n\n*Right region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) the set \\(\\{(i,j):i<j\\}\\) is a vertical rectangle of height \\(j-1\\). These \\(n-1\\) rectangles partition \\(R\\).\n\nTogether we obtain exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), one per row and one per column. Hence the bound (10) is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Conclusion for the given board  \n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
