  such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\n2. **Two disjoint regions.**  \n   For a tile \\(T=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) (rows \\(a\\le i\\le b\\), columns \\(c\\le j\\le d\\)) the condition “\\(T\\) contains no uncovered square’’ forces, for every row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\), either \\(d<p(i)\\) (the whole tile lies left of the hole in that row) or \\(c>p(i)\\) (the whole tile lies right of the hole).  \n   If a tile were left of the hole for some rows and right for others, then two consecutive rows would have holes on opposite sides of the tile; in that case the tile can be split into two smaller rectangles without increasing the total number of tiles.  \n   Consequently **there exists an optimal tiling in which every tile lies entirely either left of all holes of the rows it occupies or entirely right of them**. Hence the whole board splits into two independent parts  \n\n   \\[\n   L=\\{(i,j):j<p(i)\\},\\qquad   \n   R=\\{(i,j):j>p(i)\\},\n   \\]\n\n   and the total number of tiles equals the number used for \\(L\\) plus the number used for \\(R\\).\n\n3. **Skyline Lemma (left‑justified shapes).**  \n   Let \\(h_1,\\dots ,h_n\\ge0\\) be distinct integers and let  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le h_i\\}.\n   \\]  \n   The minimum number of axis‑parallel rectangles that partition \\(S\\) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,h_i-h_{i-1}\\},\\qquad h_0:=0.\n   \\tag{Lemma 1}\n   \\]  \n\n   *Proof.* Scan the rows from top to bottom. Whenever the height rises from \\(h_{i-1}\\) to \\(h_i\\) a new vertical rectangle must be started; when the height falls we simply stop some of the already started rectangles. The greedy construction uses exactly the displayed number of rectangles, and no construction can use fewer because each increase creates a column that does not exist above row \\(i-1\\) and therefore must be the left side of a new rectangle. ∎\n\n4. **Applying the Skyline Lemma.**  \n   In \\(L\\) the \\(i\\)-th row has length \\(\\ell_i=p(i)-1\\). Since the \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct, Lemma 1 gives  \n\n   \\[\n   N_L=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,\\ell_i-\\ell_{i-1}\\}\n        =\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,p(i)-p(i-1)\\},\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]  \n   where we set \\(p(0):=0\\).  \n\n   In \\(R\\) the rows are right‑justified; after reversing the order of columns the row lengths become \\(r_i=n-p(i)\\) (also distinct). Lemma 1 yields  \n\n   \\[\n   N_R=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,r_i-r_{i-1}\\}\n        =\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\max\\{0,p(i-1)-p(i)\\},\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]  \n   where we set \\(p(0):=n\\) (so that \\(r_0=0\\)).  \n\n   Adding (1) and (2) and using \\(\\max\\{0,x\\}+\\max\\{0,-x\\}=|x|\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   N_L+N_R\n   =p(1)+(n-p(1))+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}|p(i)-p(i-1)|\n   = (n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}|p(i)-p(i-1)|.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **A universal lower bound for permutations.**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n   \\sum_{i=2}^{n}|p(i)-p(i-1)|\\ge n-1,\n   \\]  \n   with equality only for the monotone permutations \\(p(i)=i\\) and \\(p(i)=n+1-i\\).  \n\n   *Proof.* The numbers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) appear in the sequence \\(p(1),p(2),\\dots ,p(n)\\). Between two consecutive terms the walk on the integer line must cross each of the \\(n-1\\) gaps \\(\\{k,k+1\\}\\) at least once; each crossing contributes at least \\(1\\) to the sum of absolute differences. Hence the sum is at least \\(n-1\\). Equality forces every step to be of size \\(1\\), i.e. the sequence must be strictly increasing or strictly decreasing. ∎\n\n   Substituting Lemma 2 into (3) yields the **general lower bound**\n\n   \\[\n   N_L+N_R\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because any admissible tiling can be chosen without straddling tiles (Lemma 0 below), the total number of tiles in any tiling is at least \\(2n-2\\).\n\n6. **Eliminating straddling tiles (Lemma 0).**  \n\n   *Lemma 0.* *There exists an optimal tiling (i.e. with the minimum possible number of tiles) in which every tile lies completely either in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\).*  \n\n   *Proof.* Assume a minimum‑size tiling contains a tile \\(T=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) that is not wholly in \\(L\\) nor wholly in \\(R\\). Then there are consecutive rows \\(i,i+1\\in[a,b]\\) with \\(p(i)>d\\) and \\(p(i+1)<c\\). Let \\(i_0\\) be the smallest such index. The rows \\(a,\\dots ,i_0\\) have their holes left of the tile, the rows \\(i_0+1,\\dots ,b\\) have their holes right of the tile. Split \\(T\\) into  \n\n   \\[\n   T_1=[a,i_0]\\times[c,d],\\qquad   \n   T_2=[i_0+1,b]\\times[c,d].\n   \\]  \n\n   Both \\(T_1\\) and \\(T_2\\) avoid all holes. Replace \\(T\\) by \\(T_1\\cup T_2\\); the number of tiles increases by one. However, the horizontal side that separates \\(T_1\\) from \\(T_2\\) can be merged with the neighbouring rectangle that lies on the same side of the holes for the corresponding rows (such a neighbour exists because the uncovered squares in those rows are contiguous). This merger reduces the tile count by one, restoring the original total while eliminating the straddling tile. Repeating the operation eliminates all straddling tiles without increasing the total number of tiles, contradicting the minimality of the original tiling unless no straddling tile existed. ∎\n\n   Hence the lower bound (4) applies to the whole board.\n\n7. **Achieving the bound.**  \n   Take the **identity permutation** \\(p(i)=i\\). Then \\(\\ell_i=i-1\\) and \\(r_i=n-i\\).  \n\n   - In \\(L\\) the squares form the left‑justified shape with row lengths \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\). The greedy construction of Lemma 1 yields exactly \\(n-1\\) vertical rectangles: for each column \\(j\\;(1\\le j\\le n-1)\\) the rectangle  \n     \\[\n     \\{(i,j):i>j\\}\n     \\]  \n     covers all squares of that column that lie left of the diagonal.  \n\n   - In \\(R\\) the right‑justified shape has row lengths \\(n-1,n-2,\\dots ,0\\). After reversing columns we obtain again a left‑justified shape with the same heights, giving another \\(n-1\\) vertical rectangles: for each column \\(j\\;(2\\le j\\le n)\\) the rectangle  \n     \\[\n     \\{(i,j):i<j\\}\n     \\]  \n     covers all squares of that column that lie right of the diagonal.\n\n   Altogether we use exactly \\( (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles, and the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\). Thus the bound (4) is sharp.\n\n8. **Conclusion for the given board.**  \n   With \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The solution should explicitly state \"since p>3 is prime, it's not divisible by 3, so p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\" for complete rigor. The claim is mathematically correct and the reasoning is sound, but lacks an explicit justification step. This is a presentation issue, not a mathematical error.\n\n**WRONG Classification (don't do this):**\n*   ~~**Location:** \"p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"~~\n    *   ~~**Issue:** Critical Error - This claim needs proof.~~ ❌ WRONG - This would