ered squares are \\(\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i\\neq r\\}\\).  \nRestrict the original tiling to this subboard.  \nEvery original tile either lies completely inside the subboard or is cut by the removed row or column; in the latter case it contributes at most two tiles to the restriction. Consequently the restriction uses at most \\((\\#\\text{original tiles})-2\\) tiles.\n\nBy the induction hypothesis the subboard needs at least  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)-2\n\\]\n\ntiles. Therefore the original tiling contains at least  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(2(n-1)-2\\bigr)+2 = 2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles. This completes the induction and proves:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Any admissible tiling uses }\\ge 2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An upper bound: a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nDefine the inverse permutation \\(q\\) of \\(p\\) by  \n\n\\[\nq(j)=i\\iff p(i)=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor convenience set \\(q(0)=0\\) and \\(q(n+1)=n\\).\n\n**Construction of a monotone path.**  \nStarting at the lattice point \\((0,0)\\) we perform, for each \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{(V)}&\\quad (j-1,q(j-1))\\longrightarrow (j-1,q(j)),\\\\\n\\text{(H)}&\\quad (j-1,q(j))\\longrightarrow (j,q(j)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAfter the last horizontal step we are at \\((n,q(n))\\); finally draw the vertical segment  \n\n\\[\n\\text{(V)}\\quad (n,q(n))\\longrightarrow (n,n).\n\\]\n\nAll steps lie on grid lines.  \nThe hole in column \\(j\\) is the unit square whose lower‑left corner is \\((j-1,q(j)-1)\\); the vertical segment in column \\(j-1\\) lies strictly to its left, and the horizontal segment in row \\(q(j)\\) lies on its top edge. Hence the path does **not** intersect the interior of any hole.\n\n**Rectangles derived from the path.**  \n\n*Vertical rectangles.*  \nFor each interior vertical step (there are \\(n-1\\) of them, namely those with \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)) define  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\bigl\\{(i,j)\\