".**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. Let the uncovered squares be \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) where \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) \\((n=2025)\\).  \n2. For each column \\(j\\) the set of squares **above** the uncovered one consists of exactly \\(j-1\\) unit squares; consequently each column \\(j\\ge 2\\) contains a unique *topmost* square of the upper region.  \n   Analogously each column \\(j\\le n-1\\) contains a unique *bottommost* square of the lower region.  \n3. **Lemma\u202f1.** Any axis\u2011aligned rectangle lying completely in the upper region can contain at most one topmost square.  \n   *Proof idea:* the rows occupied in column \\(j+1\\) are a superset of those in column \\(j\\); a rectangle containing two topmost squares would also contain a square that does not belong to the upper region \u2013 a contradiction.  \n4. From Lemma\u202f1 the upper region needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles; by symmetry the lower region needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles. Hence any tiling of the whole board requires at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \n5. **Construction achieving the bound.** Choose the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e. \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\).  \n   - For each column \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n     \\[\n     R_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\n     \\]\n     covering all squares above the diagonal in column \\(j\\).  \n   - For each row \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n     \\[\n     H_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n     \\]\n     covering all squares below the diagonal in row \\(i\\).  \n   The families \\(\\{R_j\\}\\) and \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) consist of \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles each, together covering every square except the diagonal.  \n6. Thus the lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is attainable, and the minimal possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this number is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nRows are indexed by \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) from top to bottom and columns by \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) from left to right.  \nExactly one unit square in each row and each column is left uncovered; denote the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) by \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\), where \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Geometry of the uncovered squares  \n\nFor a fixed column \\(j\\) consider the set  \n\n\\[\nU_j=\\{\\,i\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nA square \\((i,j)\\) lies **above** the uncovered square of its row iff \\(\\sigma(i)<j\\); therefore the squares of the upper region in column \\(j\\) are precisely \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in U_j\\}\\).  \nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection, \\(|U_j|=j-1\\) for every \\(j\\).  \nConsequently column \\(j\\) contains exactly \\(j-1\\) squares of the upper region; for \\(j\\ge 2\\) there is a unique *topmost* square of the upper region, namely  \n\n\\[\nT_j=(r_j,j),\\qquad r_j=\\max U_j .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the squares **below** the uncovered square of their row are those with \\(\\sigma(i)>j\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_j=\\{\\,i\\mid \\sigma(i)>j\\,\\},\n\\qquad |L_j|=n-j .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(j\\le n-1\\) column \\(j\\) contains a unique *bottommost* square of the lower region,\n\n\\[\nB_j=(s_j,j),\\qquad s_j=\\min L_j .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the upper region possesses exactly \\(n-1\\) topmost squares \\(\\{T_j\\mid j=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the lower region possesses exactly \\(n-1\\) bottommost squares \\(\\{B_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A rectangle can contain at most one topmost (or bottommost) square  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\text{Any axis\u2011aligned rectangle lying completely in the upper region\ncontains at most one of the squares }T_j .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a rectangle \\(R\\) be contained in the upper region and suppose it contains two distinct topmost squares\n\\(T_{j_1}=(r_{j_1},j_1)\\) and \\(T_{j_2}=(r_{j_2},j_2)\\) with \\(j_1<j_2\\).\nBecause the rectangle\u2019s row set is an interval, it contains the whole interval\n\\([\\,r_{j_1},\\,r_{j_2}\\,]\\); because its column set is an interval, it contains the whole interval \\([\\,j_1,\\,j_2\\,]\\).\nHence the cell \\((r_{j_2},j_1)\\) belongs to \\(R\\).\n\nNow \\(U_{j_1}\\subseteq U_{j_2}\\): a row belongs to \\(U_{j_2}\\) iff its uncovered column is \\(<j_2\\); this set contains all rows with uncovered column \\(<j_1\\) (the set \\(U_{j_1}\\)) and additionally the unique row \\(i_0\\) with \\(\\sigma(i_0)=j_1\\).  \nTherefore the maximal element of \\(U_{j_2}\\) satisfies \\(r_{j_2}>r_{j_1}\\); in particular the row index \\(r_{j_2}\\) does **not** belong to \\(U_{j_1}\\).  \nConsequently the square \\((r_{j_2},j_1)\\) is **not** in the upper region, contradicting the assumption that \\(R\\) is contained in the upper region. \u220e\n\nThe same argument applied to the lower region (using the sets \\(L_j\\) and the bottommost squares \\(B_j\\)) yields:\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{Any rectangle lying completely in the lower region\ncontains at most one of the squares }B_j .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEvery tile is a rectangle that lies entirely either in the upper region or in the lower region (a rectangle intersecting both would contain a diagonal square \\((i,i)\\), which is uncovered).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each tile covering the upper region can contain at most one topmost square, and there are \\(n-1\\) such squares.  \nHence at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are necessary to cover the upper region.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 the same holds for the lower region, giving another \\(n-1\\) necessary tiles.\n\nTherefore any admissible tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e. set \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen the upper region consists of all squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) and the lower region consists of all squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\).\n\n*Upper region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R_j\\) is a \\((j-1)\\times1\\) rectangle, its sides lie on grid lines, and the family \\(\\{R_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles whose union is exactly the set \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}\\).\n\n*Lower region.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_i\\) is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle, and the family \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=2}^{n}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles whose union is exactly the set \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint (one lies strictly above the diagonal, the other strictly below) and together cover every unit square except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), which are precisely the required uncovered squares.  \nThus we have a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality  \n\nEquation\u202f(3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimal possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
