ther a subset of [1, p(i)-1] or a subset of [p(i)+1, n]. However, the rectangle's column interval is the same across all rows (since rectangle is axis-aligned). So if the rectangle includes rows i and j, then the column interval must be a subset of both [1, p(i)-1] and [1, p(j)-1] (if it's left side) or a subset of both [p(i)+1, n] and [p(j)+1, n] (if it's right side). So the rectangle can be placed only on one side of all rows it covers. That is, either the rectangle is entirely left of all uncovered squares in its rows, or entirely right. So the rectangle cannot mix left and right across rows.\n\nThus the rectangle must be wholly in L or wholly in R. So the lower bound argument that each rectangle can contain at most one r_i (rightmost cell of L) is plausible. But we need to verify that the argument is correct: In L, each row i has a rightmost cell r_i = (i, p(i)-1). The rectangle's column interval is [a, c] (c is rightmost column). For rectangle to be in L, we need c <= p(i)-1 for each row i in I (the set of rows of rectangle). So c <= min_{i∈I} (p(i)-1). The rectangle contains r_i only if p(i)-1 = c. Since p(i)-1 are distinct across rows? Wait, p(i) is a permutation of {1,...,n}, so p(i)-1 yields values from 0 to n-1, but 0 corresponds to p(i)=1 (which is excluded because that row has no left region). For rows with p(i)>1, p(i)-1 are distinct values in {1,...,n-1}. Indeed, p is a permutation, so p(i)-1 are distinct for all i where p(i)>1. So each distinct column value appears at most once as a rightmost cell. So a rectangle can contain at most one r_i. So at least n-1 rectangles needed to cover all r_i's. Similarly for ℓ_i in R.\n\nThus lower bound of 2n-2 is correct.\n\nNow we need to verify that the solution's reasoning is valid: It uses Lemma 1 and symmetric argument. The lemma's proof seems correct. It uses the fact that p(i)-1 are distinct. It also uses that rectangle's rightmost column c must satisfy p(i)-1 >= c for all rows i in I, and to contain r_i we need p(i)-1 <= c, so equality. So at most one row can satisfy equality because p(i)-1 are distinct. So lemma holds.\n\nThus reasoning uses valid mathematical principles: case analysis, counting, combinatorial argument, proof by contradiction? Actually not contradiction but direct argument. It's valid.\n\nNow we need to check if any presentation issues: The solution is fairly rigorous. However, we need to see if there are any justification gaps or critical errors. The solution defines L_i and R_i, uses lemma. The lemma's proof is a bit verbose but correct. The symmetric argument for R is not fully written but claimed symmetric. That's a justification gap? The solution says \"An argument symmetric to Lemma 1 shows that any rectangle contained in R contains at most one ℓ_i.\" This is a typical approach; it's acceptable. The solution does not explicitly prove the symmetric case, but it's a standard symmetrical argument. According to the guidelines, missing a symmetric proof is a justification gap, not a critical error, because the reasoning is sound and the method is valid. So we can classify as Justification Gap.\n\nWe also need to check the construction: The solution describes horizontal tiles for each row left and right of the uncovered square. It claims they are disjoint because they are in different rows. That's correct. It counts tiles: exactly n-1 left tiles and n-1 right tiles. That yields 2n-2. The construction is explicit, providing concrete details: each tile is defined as a set of squares in a row. That's Category C (explicit specification). So Level 2 passes.\n\nThus overall answer is correct, reasoning valid, presentation has minor gaps (the symmetric argument not fully elaborated). So final verdict PASS.\n\nNow we need to produce JSON output with fields: verdict, confidence, issues, answer_correctness, reasoning.\n\nWe need to list any issues found. According to Level 3, we have a justification gap: missing explicit symmetric proof for R, but not a critica